[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To compute \(\cos 180^\circ\), we need to understand the definition of the cosine function in a right triangle. The cosine of an angle in a right triangle is defined as the ratio of the length of the adjacent side to the length of the hypotenuse. However, cosine can also be defined as the ratio of the length of the vertical side to the length of the horizontal side.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m For \(\cos 180^\circ\), the vertical side is the x-coordinate of the vertex of the angle, and the horizontal side is the y-coordinate of the vertex. At \(180^\circ\), the vertex is directly above or below the origin along the vertical axis, and at \(180^\circ\), the vertical side is 0 and the horizontal side is 0. Therefore, the cosine of \(180^\circ\) is \(\frac{0}{0}\), which is undefined. This occurs because the vertical side is approaching 0 (approaching infinity), and the horizontal side is approaching 0 (approaching infinity), and as these values get closer together, the ratio \(\frac{0}{\infty}\) becomes imaginary.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, we conclude that \(\cos 180^\circ = 0\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The final answer is \(\boxed{0}\).<|endoftext|>
