[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find \( b \) in the equation \( y = a \sin(b x + c) \), we need to identify the coefficient of \( x \) in the argument of the sine function. The general form of the sine function is \( \sin(bx + c) \), where \( \sin(x) \) has a coefficient of \( b \) in \( x \). Therefore, we need to extract \( b \) from the argument \( bx + c \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m In the given equation \( y = a \sin(bx + c) \), the argument of the sine function is \( bx + c \). Here, the coefficient in front of \( x \) is \( b \). Thus, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ b = b \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The value of \( b \) can be any positive constant if the sine function is part of a standard Fourier series or periodic function. However, since the problem does not specify a particular value for \( b \), we assume that the original equation \( y = 2 \sin(4x + \pi/2) \) is part of such a family.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The general form of the sine function \( \sin(kx + c) \) for any \( k \) and \( c \) is \( \sin(kx + c) = \sin(kx + c) = -\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{2} + kx - c\right) \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m For a positive constant \( \sin(4x + \pi/2) \), the coefficient \( b \) is simply the coefficient of \( x \) in the argument of the sine function. Therefore, \( b = 4 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the value of \( b \) is \(\boxed{4}\).<|endoftext|>
