[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Z = (0,7);
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m draw(X--Y--Z--X);
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m draw(rightanglemark(Y,X,Z,23));
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m label("$X$",X,SW);
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m label("$Y$",Y,SE);
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m label("$Z$",Z,N);
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m label("$25$",(Y+Z)/2,NE);
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m label("$24$",Y/2,S);
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m [/asy]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find \(\tan Y\), we need to determine the ratio of the opposite side to the adjacent side in the right triangle. The given triangle has a right angle at \(X\) and another angle at \(Y\). We are given the hypotenuse \(XY = 24\), the adjacent side \(XZ = 25\), and the opposite side \(YZ = 7\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, we calculate the length of the hypotenuse \(XY\) using the Pythagorean theorem:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m XY = \sqrt{XZ^2 + YZ^2} = \sqrt{25^2 + 7^2} = \sqrt{625 + 49} = \sqrt{674}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since \(\tan Y = \frac{\text{opposite}}{\text{adjacent}}\), we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \tan Y = \frac{YZ}{XZ} = \frac{7}{25}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the value of \(\tan Y\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{\frac{7}{25}}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \frac{7}{24}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
