[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Three faucets fill a 100-gallon tub in 6 minutes.  How long, in seconds, does it take six faucets to fill a 25-gallon tub?  Assume that all faucets dispense water at the same rate.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine how long it takes six faucets to fill a 25-gallon tub, we start by calculating the rate at which each faucet dispenses water. 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, we know that three faucets can fill a 100-gallon tub in 6 minutes. Therefore, the rate of water flow for three faucets is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Rate of three faucets} = \frac{100 \text{ gallons}}{6 \text{ minutes}} = \frac{100}{6} \text{ gallons per minute} = \frac{50}{3} \text{ gallons per minute}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we need to find the rate of one faucet. Since there are three faucets, the rate of one faucet is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Rate of one faucet} = \frac{50}{3} \text{ gallons per minute}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, if we have six faucets, the combined rate is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Combined rate of six faucets} = 6 \times \frac{50}{3} = 20 \text{ gallons per minute}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we need to find out how long it takes for these six faucets to fill a 25-gallon tub. We do this by dividing the volume of the tub by the combined rate of the faucets:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Time to fill the 25-gallon tub} = \frac{25 \text{ gallons}}{20 \text{ gallons per minute}} = \frac{5}{4} \text{ minutes} = 1.25 \text{ minutes}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Finally, we convert the time from minutes to seconds:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1.25 \text{ minutes} = 1.25 \times 60 \text{ seconds} = 75 \text{ seconds}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, it takes six faucets six minutes to fill a 25-gallon tub, and six minutes is equal to 75 seconds.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{75}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
