[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To compute \(\cos 240^\circ\), we can use the cosine value for a specific angle. The cosine function is periodic with a period of \(360^\circ\), so \(240^\circ\) can be written as \(120^\circ + 120^\circ = 2 \times 120^\circ\). This means we can use the cosine of \(120^\circ\) and apply the known values for the cosine of smaller angles.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The cosine of \(120^\circ\) can be found using the value for \(120^\circ\). The cosine of \(120^\circ\) is \(\cos 120^\circ = -\frac{1}{2}\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore,
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \cos 240^\circ = \cos (120^\circ + 120^\circ) = \cos 120^\circ \cos 120^\circ - \sin 120^\circ \sin 120^\circ.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We know that \(\cos 120^\circ = -\frac{1}{2}\) and \(\sin 120^\circ = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\), so we can substitute these values in:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \cos 240^\circ = \left(-\frac{1}{2}\right) \left(-\frac{1}{2}\right) - \left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right) \left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right) = \frac{1}{4} - \frac{3}{4} = -\frac{1}{2}.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the value of \(\cos 240^\circ\) is \(\boxed{-\frac{1}{2}}\).<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m [Ground Truth]
