[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m A rectangle has integer side lengths. One pair of opposite sides is increased by $30\%$ and the other pair of sides is decreased by $20\%$. The new side lengths are also integers. What is the smallest possible area, in square units, of the new rectangle?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the smallest possible area of the new rectangle, we start by defining the original dimensions of the rectangle. Let the length be \( l \) and the width be \( w \). The area of the original rectangle is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Area}_{\text{original}} = l \times w
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m After the changes, the new dimensions become:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - The length is increased by \( 30\% \), so the new length is \( 1.3l \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - The width is decreased by \( 20\% \), so the new width is \( 0.8w \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The new area of the rectangle is then:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Area}_{\text{new}} = \left(1.3l\right) \times \left(0.8w\right)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We can simplify the expression for the new area:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Area}_{\text{new}} = 1.3 \times 0.8 \times l \times w
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1.3 \times 0.8 = 1.04
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the new area is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Area}_{\text{new}} = 1.04l \times w
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the smallest possible area, we need to minimize the product \( l \times w \). Let's assume \( l = w \) for simplicity, so:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Area}_{\text{new}} = 1.04 l^2
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since \( l \) and \( w \) are integers, \( l \) can range from 1 to the largest integer less than or equal to 1.04, which is 6 (if \( l \) is an integer and \( w \) is an integer and \( l \geq w \)). If \( l = 1 \), then \( w = 1 \), and the area is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Area}_{\text{new}} = 1.04 \times 1^2 = 1.04 \text{ square units}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m If \( l = 6 \), then \( w = 6 \), and the area is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Area}_{\text{new}} = 1.04 \times 6^2 = 1.04 \times 36 = 37.44 \text{ square units}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Among these values, the smallest is \( 1.04 \) square units. Therefore, the smallest possible area of the new rectangle is:
