[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the units digit of \(23^{23}\), we need to observe the pattern in the units digits of the powers of 3. Let's compute the first few powers of 3 to see the pattern:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3^1 & = 3 \quad (\text{units digit is } 3) \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3^2 & = 9 \quad (\text{units digit is } 9) \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3^3 & = 27 \quad (\text{units digit is } 7) \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3^4 & = 81 \quad (\text{units digit is } 1) \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3^5 & = 243 \quad (\text{units digit is } 3) \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We can see that the units digits repeat every 4 numbers: 3, 9, 7, 1. Therefore, to find the units digit of \(3^{23}\), we need to determine the position of 23 in the repeating cycle. We do this by finding the remainder when 23 is divided by 4:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 23 \div 4 = 5 \text{ remainder } 3
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The remainder is 3, which means that 23 is in the third position of the repeating cycle. Thus, the units digit of \(3^{23}\) is the same as the units digit of \(3^3\), which is 7.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the units digit of \(23^{23}\) is \(\boxed{7}\).<|endoftext|>
