[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m It took $4$ days for $75$ workers, all working together at the same rate, to build an embankment. If only $50$ workers had been available, how many total days would it have taken to build the embankment?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine how many total days it would have taken 50 workers to build the embankment, we need to first calculate the total amount of work required. According to the problem, 75 workers can build the embankment in 4 days. Therefore, the total work (or effort) required to build the embankment is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 75 \text{ workers} \times 4 \text{ days} = 300 \text{ worker-days}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we need to determine how many days 50 workers would take to complete the same amount of work. Let's denote the number of days required by 50 workers to build the embankment as \(d\). Since the total work is 300 worker-days, we can set up the following equation:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 50 \text{ workers} \times d \text{ days} = 300 \text{ worker-days}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find \(d\), we solve the equation by dividing both sides by 50:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m d = \frac{300 \text{ worker-days}}{50 \text{ workers}} = 6 \text{ days}
