[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The lengths in feet of three pieces of timber are 48, 72, and 40. The sawmill operator needs to cut the timber into logs of equal length with no waste. How many feet long is the greatest possible length she can cut?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the greatest possible length the sawmill operator can cut, we need to find the least common multiple (LCM) of the lengths 48, 72, and 40, since this will give us the smallest length that is a common divisor of all three numbers, and thus it will be the greatest length she can cut.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We start by finding the prime factorization of each number:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \(48 = 2^4 \times 3\)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \(72 = 2^3 \times 3^2\)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \(40 = 2^3 \times 5\)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The LCM is found by taking the highest power of each prime that appears in the factorizations:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - The highest power of 2 is \(2^4\)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - The highest power of 3 is \(3^2\)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - The highest power of 5 is \(5^1\)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, the LCM is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \text{LCM} = 2^4 \times 3^2 \times 5 = 16 \times 9 \times 5 = 720 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This means the greatest possible length she can cut is 720 feet. To verify, we can check how many pieces of 720 feet each can be cut from each piece of timber:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - From 48 feet: \(48 \div 720 = 0\) pieces
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - From 72 feet: \(72 \div 720 = 0\) pieces
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - From 40 feet: \(40 \div 720 = 0\) pieces
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since the LCM is the smallest length that satisfies all three conditions, the greatest possible length the sawmill operator can cut is indeed \(\boxed{720}\) feet.<|endoftext|>
