[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m John ran at a speed of 6 miles per hour for 2 hours, then stopped for 1 hour before going another 3 hours at the same speed. How many kilometers did he run in total?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the total distance John ran, we need to calculate the distance he covers during each of the two 2-hour periods and then sum these distances with the distance covered during the third 3-hour period.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, let's calculate the distance covered during the first 2 hours:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \text{Distance} = \text{Speed} \times \text{Time} = 6 \, \text{miles per hour} \times 2 \, \text{hours} = 12 \, \text{miles} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we calculate the distance covered during the third 3-hour period:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \text{Distance} = \text{Speed} \times \text{Time} = 6 \, \text{miles per hour} \times 3 \, \text{hours} = 18 \, \text{miles} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, we sum the distances from the three periods to get the total distance John ran:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \text{Total Distance} = 12 \, \text{miles} + 18 \, \text{miles} = 30 \, \text{miles} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the total distance John ran is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \boxed{30} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
