[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To solve this problem, we need to understand the properties of reflections and how they affect points in the coordinate plane. A reflection over the x-axis takes a point \((x, y)\) and reflects it to \((x, -y)\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Given the original point \((3, 4)\), we apply the reflection over the x-axis. The coordinates of the point after reflection will be \((3, -4)\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Here is the step-by-step reasoning:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. Identify the original coordinates: \((3, 4)\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. Apply the reflection transformation: Reflecting over the x-axis means changing the sign of the y-coordinate while keeping the x-coordinate the same.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3. The new coordinates after the reflection will be \((3, -4)\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the coordinates of the image of the point \((3, 4)\) after the reflection in the x-axis are \(\boxed{(3, -4)}\).<|endoftext|>
