[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m A pyramid is formed on a $6\times 8$ rectangular base. The four edges joining the apex to the corners of the rectangular base each have length $13$. What is the volume of the pyramid?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the volume of the pyramid, we need to determine the height of the pyramid, which is equal to the length of one of the edges that join the apex to the corners of the rectangular base. The base of the rectangle is given as \(6 \times 8 = 48\) units.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Each non-adjacent edge in a square pyramid has a length of \(13\). Since there is a right angle at the apex, the length of the diagonal of the compressed square base can be calculated as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. Recognize that the compressed square base can be broken down into a rectangle and a right triangle. Each side of the compressed square base is \(13\) units long, so the length of the compressed rectangle is \(48 - 2 \times 13 = 28\) units.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. The diagonal of the compressed square base can be calculated using the Pythagorean theorem. The diagonal \(d\) of a square with side length \(s\) is given by \(d = s\sqrt{2}\). Here, \(s = 13\), so the diagonal is \(13\sqrt{2}\) units.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3. The height \(h\) of the pyramid can be found using the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle formed by splitting the compressed square into a rectangle and a right triangle. The height splits the diagonal into two equal segments of length \(13\sqrt{2}/2\). Therefore, the height \(h\) is \(13\sqrt{2}/2\) units.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 4. The volume \(V\) of a pyramid is given by the formula \(V = \frac{1}{3} \times \text{Base Area} \times \text{Height}\). The base area of the pyramid is the area of the rectangle (compressed square base) which is \(28 \times 13 = 364\) square units.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 5. Substituting the height and base area into the formula for volume, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    V = \frac{1}{3} \times 364 \times \frac{13\sqrt{2}}{2} = \frac{1}{3} \times 364 \times 6.5\sqrt{2} = 628\sqrt{2}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the volume of the pyramid is \(\boxed{628\sqrt{2}}\).<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 192
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m [Is Boxed Matched]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m True
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m [Extracted Answer]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 628\sqrt{2}
