[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Margaret started with 72 ice cream cones. By noon, she had \(\frac{2}{3}\) as many cones as she had at the beginning, which means she had \(72 \times \frac{2}{3} = 48\) ice cream cones by noon. By the end of the day, she had \(\frac{2}{3}\) as many cones as she had at noon, which means she had \(48 \times \frac{2}{3} = 32\) ice cream cones by the end of the day.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Finally, to find out how many ice cream cones she has at the end of the day, we add the number of ice cream cones she had at the beginning and the end of the day. So, she has 72 ice cream cones at the beginning of the day, 48 ice cream cones by noon, and 32 ice cream cones by the end of the day, making a total of \(72 + 48 + 32\).
