[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 50 = 2b^2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ b^2 = 25 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ b = 5 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ### Step 3: Calculate the Radius of the Circle
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The radius of the circle is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ a - c = 5 - 5 = 0 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This information seems incorrect because it doesn't provide an actual radius. We need to verify if any other conditions are met or if we made a mistake. However, the primary focus lies at the center of the ellipse, which is the origin. So, the radius of the circle centered at the intersection of the ellipse with the coordinate axes (the line \(x = y\)) is actually the distance from the origin to the x-axis or y-axis, which is 5.
