[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine how many three-digit positive integers there are, we need to consider the possible values for the hundreds, tens, and units digits of these integers. A three-digit integer ranges from 100 to 999.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. **Units Digit (unit):**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    The units digit (unit) can be any digit from 0 to 9. This means there are 10 possible choices for the units digit.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. **Hundreds Digit (hundred):**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    The hundreds digit (hundred) can also be any digit from 0 to 9. This means there are 10 possible choices for the hundreds digit.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3. **Tens Digit (ten):**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    The tens digit (ten) can also be any digit from 0 to 9. This means there are 10 possible choices for the tens digit.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, we multiply the number of choices for each digit to find the total number of three-digit positive integers:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 10 \text{ (choices for hundreds digit)} \times 10 \text{ (choices for tens digit)} \times 10 \text{ (choices for units digit)} = 1000
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the total number of three-digit positive integers is \(\boxed{1000}\).<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
