[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Find the matrix $\mathbf{P}$ such that for any vector $\mathbf{v},$ $\mathbf{P} \mathbf{v}$ is the projection of $\mathbf{v}$ onto the $yz$-plane.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the matrix \(\mathbf{P}\) such that for any vector \(\mathbf{v}\), the projection of \(\mathbf{v}\) onto the \(yz\)-plane is given by \(\mathbf{P} \mathbf{v}\), we need to determine the projection of \(\mathbf{v}\) onto the \(yz\)-plane.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The projection of a vector \(\mathbf{v}\) onto the \(yz\)-plane is simply \(\mathbf{v}\). This is because any vector \(\mathbf{v}\) in a plane (whether it's diagonal, linear, or skew to any given plane) will project to the same vector that lies in its own plane.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the matrix \(\mathbf{P}\) that projects any vector onto the \(yz\)-plane is the identity matrix:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \mathbf{P} = \mathbf{I} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, the answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
