[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the value of \( b \) for the sinusoidal function \( y = a \sin(bx + c) \), we need to determine the phase shift of the function. The general form of a sinusoidal function is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ y = a \sin (bx + c) \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The phase shift of the sine function \( \sin (bx + c) \) is given by \( -\frac{c}{b} \). Notice that in the given function, the phase shift is \( \frac{\pi}{2} \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ b = 2 \]
