[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. Determine your running speed:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    Since you take 18 minutes to run 2 miles, your speed can be calculated using formula \( \text{Speed} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Time}} \). For 2 miles and 18 minutes, your speed is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \text{Speed} = \frac{2 \text{ miles}}{18 \text{ minutes}} = \frac{2}{18} \text{ miles per minute} = \frac{1}{9} \text{ miles per minute}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. Determine the time it takes to run to your friend's house:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    Your friend's house is 1 mile away, and your speed is \(\frac{1}{9}\) miles per minute. Therefore, the time it takes to run 1 mile at this speed is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \text{Time} = \frac{1 \text{ mile}}{\frac{1}{9} \text{ miles per minute}} = 1 \text{ mile} \times 9 \text{ minutes per mile} = 9 \text{ minutes}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, it will take you \boxed{9} minutes to run from your house to your friend's house.<|endoftext|>
