[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the measure of angle \(P\) in degrees, we need to use the fact that the sum of the angles in a triangle is always \(180^\circ\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Given:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - Angle \(P\) is the angle between sides \(C\) and \(D\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - Angle \(92^\circ\) is adjacent to side \(C\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - Angle \(113^\circ\) is adjacent to side \(D\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Let's denote the measure of angle \(C\) by \(A\) and the measures of angles \(P\), \(D\), and \(B\) by \(B\), \(C\), and \(A\) respectively. Note that angle \(A\) is the supplement of angle \(P\), but the measure of \(A\) cannot be determined without additional information, so we assume it might be \(180^\circ - P\).
