[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To solve this problem, we need to determine the angle measure (often represented as a fraction of a full circle, 360 degrees) that corresponds to the angle \(ACB\) in the pie chart. The points \(A\), \(B\), and \(C\) are defined as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \(A\) is the starting point, \(C\) is the ending point.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - The line segments \(AC\) and \(BC\) are vertical lines.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Given the points:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \(A\) is at the origin \((0,0)\)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \(C\) is at any posifxe point \((12, -\infty)\)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \(B\) is 20 units to the right of \(A\), so \(B\) is at \((12, 20)\)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We need to find the angle \(\angle ACB\). Since \(\angle ACB\) is subtended by the arc \(AC\) and two segments of the vertical line from \(A\) and \(B\) to reach \(C\), we can use the formula for the angle subtended by an arc in a circle:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \theta = \frac{\text{length of the arc}}{\text{radius}}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Here, the radius \(r\) is the horizontal distance from \(A\) to \(C\), which is 12 units. The arc \(AC\) is the one subtended by the angle \(\angle ACB\). If we consider \( \angle ACB = \theta \), then:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \theta = \frac{12}{12} \cdot \theta = \theta
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, any angle \(\theta\) will satisfy the relationship. However, we need a specific example that fits our context, so let's assume \(B\) is at \((12, 20)\) and \(C\) is at \((12, 20 + 20) = (0, 40)\). Then:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \angle ACB = 90^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This is because \(\angle ACB\) is the angle subtended by a semicircle (one less than a straight line) with \(A\) and \(C\) as endpoints.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the lesser measure in degrees of angle \(ACB\) is:
