[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Explain the difference between the definitions of “mean” and “average.” The mean and the average are both important measurements of central tendency, but they are used in different contexts and have different definitions. The mean, also known as the geometric mean, is calculated by averaging all of a set of numbers to get the middle value. It is represented by the Greek letter μ (mu). The average, on the other hand, is calculated by dividing the sum of a set of numbers by the number of terms in the set, to get the average value. It is represented by the Greek letter A (a), and is commonly referred to as the arithmetic mean. The mean and the average provide different insights into the central tendencies of a set of data, given that the mean, in general, is less affected by extreme values than the average, and is typically used when one wants to highlight a mathematical pattern or tendency in a data set.
