[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the degree of the product of two polynomials \( p(t) \) and \( q(t) \), we need to consider the degrees of the individual polynomials \( p(t) \) and \( q(t) \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. **Identify the degrees of the polynomials:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - \( p(t) \) is a seventh-degree polynomial, which means its highest degree term is \( a_7 t^7 \), where \( a_7 \) is a non-zero constant.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - \( q(t) \) is also a seventh-degree polynomial, so its highest degree term is \( a_7 t^7 \), where \( a_7 \) is a non-zero constant.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. **Determine the degree of the product polynomial:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The degree of the product of two polynomials is the sum of the degrees of the individual polynomials. This is because multiplying polynomials by a constant results in multiplying the coefficients of the constant separately.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3. **Calculate the sum of the degrees:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The degree of \( p(t) \) is 7.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The degree of \( q(t) \) is 7.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - Therefore, the degree of \( p(t) \cdot q(t) \) is \( 7 + 7 = 14 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 4. **State the final answer:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The degree of the product \( p(t) \cdot q(t) \) is \(\boxed{14}\).<|endoftext|>
