[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m If $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are both seventh-degree polynomials in $t$, what is the degree of $p(t)\cdot q(t)$?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the degree of the product \( p(t) \cdot q(t) \), we need to consider the degrees of the individual polynomials \( p(t) \) and \( q(t) \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m A polynomial of degree \( n \) in \( t \) is a trinomial, \( q(t) \) which is a seventh-degree polynomial. The degree of a polynomial is the highest power of \( t \) that appears in the polynomial. For example, if \( q(t) \) is a trinomial of degree \( d \), then \( q(t) \) is a polynomial of degree \( d \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The degree of the product of two polynomials is found by taking the highest degree of each polynomial. Since \( p(t) \) is a seventh-degree polynomial and each factor \( q(t) \) is a linear polynomial (degree 1), the degree of \( p(t) \cdot q(t) \) will be the sum of the degrees of \( q(t) \) and \( p(t) \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Here's the step-by-step reasoning:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. Identify the degree of \( p(t) \). Since it is a seventh-degree polynomial, its degree is 7.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. Identify the degree of \( q(t) \). Since it is a seventh-degree polynomial, its degree is 7.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3. Since the degrees of the two polynomials are identical, the degree of their product \( p(t) \cdot q(t) \) is simply the sum of the degrees of \( p(t) \) and \( q(t) \). Therefore, the degree of \( p(t) \cdot q(t) \) is 7 + 7 = 14.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, the degree of \( p(t) \cdot q(t) \) is \(\boxed{14}\).<|endoftext|>
