[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the integer \( n \) such that \( 0 \le n \le 5 \) and \( n \equiv -3736 \pmod{6} \), we need to determine the remainder when \(-3736\) is divided by \(6\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, let's find the remainder when \(3736\) is divided by \(6\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3736 \div 6 = 622 \text{ with a remainder of } 4.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3736 = 6 \times 622 + 4.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This means that:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3736 \equiv 4 \pmod{6}.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since \(-3736\) is the same as \(3736\) modulo \(6\), we can directly substitute:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m -n \equiv -3736 \pmod{6}.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This simplifies to:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m n \equiv \text{a multiple of } 6 \pmod{6}.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The only multiple of \(6\) in the range \(0 \le n \le 5\) is \(0\). Therefore:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m n = 0.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To verify, we substitute \(n = 0\) into the original modular equation:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 0 \equiv -3736 \pmod{6}.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since \(-3736 \equiv 0 \pmod{6}\), the equation holds true. Thus, the solution is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{0}.
