[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Find the integer $n,$ $-90 \le n \le 90,$ such that $\sin n^\circ = \sin 419^\circ.$
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m nangrate to the equation: sin(n)° = sin(419°).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To solve this, we need to find an integer \( n \) such that \( -90 \leq n \leq 90 \) and \(\sin(n^\circ) = \sin(419^\circ)\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, we note that the sine function has a periodicity of \(360^\circ\). This means that \(\sin(419^\circ)\) will have the same sine value as \(\sin(360^\circ - 419^\circ)\), which is \(\sin(-59^\circ)\) if \( -90 \leq n \leq 90 \). Since sine is an odd function, \(\sin(-59^\circ) = -\sin(59^\circ)\). Therefore, we need to find an \( n \) such that \(\sin(n^\circ) = -\sin(59^\circ)\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The sine function is negative in the third and fourth quadrants. However, since \( n \) must be between -90 and 90, we only need to consider the second and third quadrants (i.e., \( 0 \leq n < 360 \)).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m In the second quadrant, \(\sin(n^\circ) = \sin(180^\circ - n^\circ) = \sin(n^\circ)\). But since \(\sin(59^\circ)\) is positive and less than 1, we need to find the angle \( n \) such that \(\sin(n^\circ) = -\sin(59^\circ)\). This additional angle would need to be within the range \( 0^\circ < n \leq 360^\circ \) and satisfy the trigonometric identity.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m However, since \(\sin(n^\circ) = \sin(180^\circ - n^\circ)\) and \(\sin(n^\circ) = \sin(360^\circ + n^\circ)\) and so on, we can see that the only way the equation can be satisfied is if \( n = 59 \), because \(\sin(180^\circ - 59^\circ) = \sin(121^\circ) \approx 0.857\), and \(\sin(n^\circ) = \sin(180^\circ - n^\circ)\) will give us the negative sine value.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the integer \( n \) that satisfies the equation is \( n = 59 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{59}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
