[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the degree of \( p(t) \cdot q(t) \), we need to consider the degrees of the individual polynomials \( p(t) \) and \( q(t) \). Both \( p(t) \) and \( q(t) \) are seventh-degree polynomials, which means they both have a degree of 7.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The degree of the product of two polynomials is the sum of the degrees of the individual polynomials. Therefore, we add the degree of \( p(t) \) to the degree of \( q(t) \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Degree of } p(t) \cdot q(t) = \text{Degree of } p(t) + \text{Degree of } q(t) = 7 + 7 = 14
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the degree of \( p(t) \cdot q(t) \) is \(\boxed{14}\).<|endoftext|>
