[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To solve the expression \(\dfrac{3 \times 4}{6}\), we need to follow the order of operations, often remembered by the acronym PEMDAS (Parentheses, Exponents, Multiplication and Division (from left to right), Addition and Subtraction (from left to right)).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Step 1: Identify and perform multiplication inside the parentheses.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The expression inside the parentheses is \(3 \times 4\). This results in:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[3 \times 4 = 12\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, the expression now becomes:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[\dfrac{12}{6}\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Step 2: Perform the division.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The expression \(\dfrac{12}{6}\) is already in its simplest form, so we just need to perform the division:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[12 \div 6 = 2\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the value of \(\dfrac{3 \times 4}{6}\) is \(\boxed{2}\).<|endoftext|>
