[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m In a certain state there are 10 odd numbered roads and 15 even numbered roads. If one of these roads is chosen randomly (which is independent of the other 9 roads), calculate the probability that it is an odd-numbered road. To find the probability that a randomly chosen road is an odd-numbered road, we need to use the concept of probability. There are a total of \(10 + 15 = 25\) roads. The probability \(P\) that a randomly chosen road is an odd-numbered road is given by the ratio of the number of odd-numbered roads to the total number of roads. Mathematically, this is expressed as:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ P = \frac{\text{Number of odd-numbered roads}}{\text{Total number of roads}} = \frac{10}{25} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We can simplify this fraction to:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ P = \frac{2}{5} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Let's use Python to confirm this result.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ```python
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m # Total number of roads
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m total_roads = 10 + 15
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m # Number of odd-numbered roads
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m odd_roads = 10
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m # Probability that a randomly chosen road is an odd-numbered road
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m probability_odd = odd_roads / total_roads
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m print(probability_odd)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ```
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ```output
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 0.4
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ```
