[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. **Understand the Geometry:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - \(M\) and \(N\) are the midpoints of \(BC\) and \(AC\) respectively.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The perpendicular bisectors of \(BC\) and \(AC\) intersect at a point \(O\) inside the triangle.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - \(\angle C = 47^\circ\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. **Properties of Perpendicular Bisectors:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The perpendicular bisector of a segment is the unique line that intersects the two sides of the segment perpendicularly and is equidistant from both sides.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - Since \(M\) and \(N\) are midpoints, \(MN\) is parallel to \(BC\) and \(AC\) and is half the length of both. Thus, \(M\) and \(N\) are equidistant from \(C\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3. **Angles between Perpendicular Bisectors:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The angle between the perpendicular bisectors of \(BC\) and \(AC\) is \(90^\circ\). This is because the perpendicular bisector of a line segment creates a right angle with the segment itself.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - Therefore, \(\angle C \angle AMO = 47^\circ\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 4. **Conclusion:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - Since \(M\) and \(N\) are midpoints and lie on the perpendicular bisectors, the measure of \(\angle MON\) is half of \(\angle C \angle AMO\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - Given \(\angle C = 47^\circ\), we have \(\angle MON = \frac{47^\circ}{2} = 23^\circ\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the measure of \(\angle MON\) is \(\boxed{23}\).<|endoftext|>
