[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. **Targeted sectors of an ellipse:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    This problem is related to calculating the area of an ellipse. 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - We need to find:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      \text{Area of the trapezoid} = \frac{1}{2} \times (\text{Sum of top bases}) \times \text{Height of the trapezoid}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The heights from the bases to the trapezoid are perpendicular to the bases at the same distance from them.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - We can utilize trigonometric relationships to find the heights from the bases to the trapezoid.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3. **Determine the height from the base of 7 to the center of the trapezoid:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \text{Height from } 7 \text{ to } \text{Center} = \sqrt{13^2 - 8^2} = \sqrt{169 - 64} = \sqrt{105}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \text{Height from } 17 \text{ to } \text{Center} = \sqrt{13^2 - 17^2} = \sqrt{169 - 289} = \sqrt{-120}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    Since this height is negative, we need to consider the full radius. Let the radius be \(r\). Then:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \text{Height from } 7 \text{ to } r = \sqrt{129 - r^2}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    Since the height must be positive:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \sqrt{129 - r^2} = 18
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    Squaring both sides:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    129 - r^2 = 324
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    -r^2 = 295
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    r^2 = 295
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 4. **Determine the height from the base of 17 to the center of the trapezoid:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    Using the same logic:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \text{Height from } 17 \text{ to } \text{Center} = \sqrt{129 - r^2} = \sqrt{295}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \text{Height from } 17 \text{ to } 13 = r + 10 = \sqrt{129 - r^2}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    Given the height and radius:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \sqrt{129 - r^2} = \sqrt{295}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    129 - r^2 = 295
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    r^2 = 266
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    r = \sqrt{266}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
