[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the roots of the cubic equation \(x^3 - 3x^2 - 10x + 24 = 0\), we will use the Rational Root Theorem and synthetic division.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ### Step 1: Rational Root Theorem
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The Rational Root Theorem states that any potential rational root, expressed in the form \(\frac{p}{q}\), must have \(p\) as a factor of the constant term (24) and \(q\) as a factor of the leading coefficient (1). Therefore, the possible rational roots are \(\pm 1, \pm 2, \pm 3, \pm 4, \pm 6, \pm 8, \pm 12, \pm 24\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ### Step 2: Testing possible roots
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We will test these possible roots using synthetic division.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m #### Testing \(x = 2\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \begin{array}{r|rrrr}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2 & 1 & -3 & -10 & 24 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m   &   &  2 & 0  & -24 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \hline
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m   & 1 & -1 & -10 & 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \end{array}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since the remainder is 0, \(x = 2\) is a root.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ### Step 3: Polynomial division
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m With \(x = 2\) as a root, we can factor \(x - 2\) out of the polynomial \(x^3 - 3x^2 - 10x + 24\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m #### Polynomial division:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \begin{array}{r|rrrr}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2 & 1 & -3 & -10 & 24 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m   &   & 2 & 0 & -24 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \hline
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m   & 1 & -1 & -10 & 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \end{array}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The quotient is \(x^2 - x - 12\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ### Step 4: Solving the quadratic equation
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, we need to solve the quadratic equation \(x^2 - x - 12 = 0\). We can do this using the quadratic formula \(x = \frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\), where \(a = 1\), \(b = -1\), and \(c = -12\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m x = \frac{-(-1) \pm \sqrt{(-1)^2 - 4 \cdot 1 \cdot (-12)}}{2 \cdot 1}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m x = \frac{1 \pm \sqrt{1 + 48}}{2}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m x = \frac{1 \pm \sqrt{49}}{2}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m x = \frac{1 \pm 7}{2}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This gives us two solutions:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m x = \frac{1 + 7}{2} = \frac{8}{2} = 4
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m x = \frac{1 - 7}{2} = \frac{-6}{2} = -3
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ### Final Answer
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The roots of the cubic equation \(x^3 - 3x^2 - 10x + 24 = 0\) are:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{2, 4, -3}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
