[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the phase shift of the graph of \( y = \sin(3x - \pi) \), we need to understand how the argument of the sine function affects its phase shift.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The general form of the sine function is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ y = \sin(bx - c) \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m where:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \( b \) affects the period of the sine function, providing a scaling effect on the frequency and amplitude.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \( c \) affects the phase shift, providing a vertical shift.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m For the given function \( y = \sin(3x - \pi) \), we can identify:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \( b = 3 \)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \( c = \pi \)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The phase shift of a sine function \( y = \sin(bx - c) \) is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \text{Phase shift} = \frac{c}{b} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Substituting the values for \( b \) and \( c \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \text{Phase shift} = \frac{\pi}{3} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the phase shift of the graph of \( y = \sin(3x - \pi) \) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \boxed{\frac{\pi}{3}} \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m -\frac{\pi}{3}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
