[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ### Step 1: Define the polynomial and its roots
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Given the polynomial \(x^4 - bx^3 - x^2 + c = 0\), we can find its roots. The roots will be rational because the coefficients of the polynomial are rational. For simplicity, let's assume that \(x_1\) and \(x_2\) are the real roots of the polynomial, and \(x_3\) and \(x_4\) are the complex roots. By Vieta's formulas, we know:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - The sum of the roots \(x_1 + x_2 + x_3 + x_4 = b\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - The sum of the product of the roots taken two at a time \(x_1x_2 + x_1x_3 + x_1x_4 + x_2x_3 + x_2x_4 + x_3x_4 = -1\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - The product of the roots \(x_1x_2x_3x_4 = -c\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ### Step 2: Define the antiderivatives of \(f(x) = 2x^2 + 3x + 5\)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m A function \(f(x)\) has an antiderivative \(F(x)\) such that \(F'(x) = f(x)\). To find an antiderivative of \(f(x)\), we can use the formula:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ F(x) = F(x) - \int_0^x f(t) \, dt \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
