[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the polynomial \( f(x) \) such that \( g(f(x)) = x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 \) with \( g(x) = x^2 - 11x + 30 \), we need to first determine the roots of \( g(x) \). The roots of \( g(x) \) can be found by solving the quadratic equation:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ x^2 - 11x + 30 = 0. \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We can factorize this quadratic equation as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ x^2 - 11x + 30 = (x - 5)(x - 6) = 0. \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, the roots of \( g(x) \) are \( x = 5 \) and \( x = 6 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we substitute these roots into the polynomial \( f(x) \) to get the polynomial \( f(x) \) with complex conjugate roots. Therefore, \( f(5) \) and \( f(6) \) will be real.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, we evaluate \( f(5) \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ f(5) = 5^2 - 11 \cdot 5 + 30 = 25 - 55 + 30 = 0. \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, \( f(5) = 0 \), which means \( x = 5 \) is a root of \( f(x) \). Therefore, \( f(x) \) is a polynomial of the form:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ f(x) = (x - 5)\cdot Q(x), \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m where \( Q(x) \) is a polynomial of degree 2. Now we need to find the quadratic polynomial \( Q(x) \) such that:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ g(f(x)) = x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42. \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We substitute \( f(x) = (x - 5)Q(x) \) into \( g(x) \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ g(f(x)) = g((x - 5)Q(x)) = ((x - 5)(x - 5)Q(x))^2 - 11(x - 5)Q(x) + 30Q(x). \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We know that \( g(f(x)) = x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 \), so we need:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ ((x - 5)(x - 5)Q(x))^2 - 11(x - 5)Q(x) + 30Q(x) = x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42. \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This equation must hold for all \( x \), so we can compare coefficients of \( x^3 \), \( x^2 \), \( x \), and the constant term. The coefficient of \( x^3 \) on the left is \(0 \oplus 0 + (-11)(-1) = 11 \), and on the right, it is 0, so there is no \( x^3 \) term. The coefficient of \( x^2 \) on the left is \( 0 \oplus (-11) + (-11) = 0 - 11 = -11 \oplus -11 = 0 \), and on the right, it is \( 0 \), so there is no \( x^2 \) term. The coefficient of \( x \) on the left is \( 0 \oplus 0 + 9 = 0 \oplus 0 = 0 \), and on the right, it is \( 0 \) if we assume \( Q(x) = -x^2 + 5x \), so there is a \( -x \) term on the right. The constant term on the left is \( 0 \oplus 0 + 42 = 0 \oplus 42 = 42 \oplus 0 = 42 \), and on the right, it is 42, so there is no constant term.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the only equation that fits is \( -x = 42 - 10 \cdot 5 = 22 \). So, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ -x = 22 \implies x = -22. \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the polynomial \( f(x) \) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ f(x) = (x - 5)(-x^2 + 5x). \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We can factor out \( x \) from the expression:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ f(x) = x(-x + 5)(-x + 5) = (x - 5)^2(-x + 5) = (-x + 5)^3. \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the polynomial \( f(x) \) is:
