[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ x = 107 \cdot 109^5 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the exponent of \( 109 \) in the prime factorization of \( x^{11} \), we need to determine how many times \( 109 \) appears in the prime factorization of \( x^{11} \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We can use the property of exponents that states:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ (a \cdot b)^n = a^n \cdot b^n \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Here, \( a = 107 \), \( b = 109 \), and \( n = 11 \). Applying the exponent rule above, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ (107 \cdot 109)^{11} = 107^{11} \cdot 109^{11} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We are interested in the exponent of \( 109 \), so we focus on \( 109^{11} \). The exponent of \( 109 \) is 11.
