[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To simplify the expression \(\tan 9^\circ + \cot 9^\circ - \tan 27^\circ - \cot 27^\circ\), we will use some trigonometric identities and properties.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, recall the identity for \(\tan (90^\circ - x) = \cot x\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \tan 9^\circ = \cot 81^\circ \quad \text{and} \quad \tan 27^\circ = \cot 36^\circ.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the expression becomes:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \tan 9^\circ + \cot 9^\circ - \tan 27^\circ - \cot 27^\circ = \cot 81^\circ + \cot 9^\circ - \cot 36^\circ - \cot 27^\circ.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we use the identity for the cotangent of a sum:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \cot A + \cot B = \frac{\sin(A + B)}{\cos A \cos B}.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Applying this identity to \(\cot 9^\circ + \cot 36^\circ\) and \(\cot 36^\circ + \cot 27^\circ\), we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \cot 9^\circ + \cot 36^\circ = \frac{\sin(9^\circ + 36^\circ)}{\cos 9^\circ \cos 36^\circ} = \frac{\sin 45^\circ}{\cos 9^\circ \cos 36^\circ} = \frac{\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}}{\cos 9^\circ \cos 36^\circ} = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2 \cos 9^\circ \cos 36^\circ},
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m and
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
