[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the remainder when \(2x^6 - x^4 + 4x^2 - 7\) is divided by \(x^2 + 4x + 3\), we can use the method of polynomial long division or the Remainder Theorem. The Remainder Theorem states that the remainder of the division of a polynomial \(f(x)\) by a linear divisor \(x - c\) is \(f(c)\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Here, we need to divide \(2x^6 - x^4 + 4x^2 - 7\) by \(x^2 + 4x + 3\). The linear divisor is \(x - 3\). So, we set \(c = 3\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, we perform the division:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \begin{array}{r|rrrrrrr}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3 & 2 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -7 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    &   & 6 & 12 & 44 & 162 & 1218 & \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \hline
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    & 2 & 6 & 12 & 44 & 162 & -1212 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \end{array}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, the remainder when \(2x^6 - x^4 + 4x^2 - 7\) is divided by \(x^2 + 4x + 3\) is \(-1212\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the answer is \(\boxed{-1212}\).<|endoftext|>
