[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine which of the five segments shown is the longest, we need to analyze the angles they subtend on the unit circle. The vertex angle of a sector of a circle with angle \( \theta \) is given by \( 180^\circ - \theta \). For \( \angle ACB = 55^\circ \), the corresponding sector angle \( \alpha \) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \alpha = 180^\circ - 55^\circ = 125^\circ \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The lengths of the arcs subtended by the angles will be proportional to the length of the segments they cross. The longest segment corresponds to the arc that subtends the largest angle. For an angle of \( 125^\circ \), the largest angle in a sector is \( 180^\circ - 40^\circ = 140^\circ \), so the longest segment is the arc between \( A \) and \( B \) when the angle is \( \alpha = 125^\circ \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the length of the arc \( MBQ \), we use the formula for arc length \( s = r \theta \), where \( r \) is the radius and \( \theta \) is the angle in radians. First, we convert \( 125^\circ \) to radians:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 125^\circ = \frac{125 \pi}{180} = \frac{5\pi}{12} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The radius \( r \) of the circle is the distance from the center to the point of intersection on the unit circle, which is \( \sqrt{3} \). Therefore, the length of the arc \( MBQ \) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ s = r \theta = \sqrt{3} \cdot \frac{5\pi}{12} = \frac{5\pi \sqrt{3}}{12} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The other segments are smaller arcs:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. Length of \( AC \): \( \sqrt{3} \cdot 5.5 = \frac{5\sqrt{3}}{2} \)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. Length of \( CD \): \( \sqrt{3} \cdot 0.5 = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3. Length of \( DB \): \( \sqrt{3} \cdot (-0.5) = -\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \) (not relevant for this calculation)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 4. Length of \( CA \): \( \sqrt{3} \cdot 2 = 2\sqrt{3} \)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 5. Length of \( BD \): \( \sqrt{3} \cdot 0.5 = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The lengths of the other segments are all smaller than \( \frac{5\sqrt{3}}{2} \) and \( \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \), so the longest segment is the arc \( MBQ \) which subtends an angle of \( 125^\circ \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the longest segment is the arc between \( A \) and \( B \), which is the green segment:
