[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the number of distinct positive integers that can be represented as the difference of two numbers in the set \(\{1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13\}\), we need to consider all possible pairs \((a, b)\) where \(a\) and \(b\) are elements of the set such that \(a \neq b\) and \(a > b\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, let's list all possible pairs \((a, b)\) and determine which of them make \(a\) minus \(b\) a positive integer:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - If \(a = 1\), then \(b\) can be 3 (since \(1 - 3 < 0\)), 5 (since \(1 - 5 < 0\)), 7 (since \(1 - 7 < 0\)), 9 (since \(1 - 9 < 0\)), 11 (since \(1 - 11 < 0\)), 13 (since \(1 - 13 < 0\)), which all result in negative numbers. Therefore, no such pair exists.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - If \(a = 2\), then \(b\) can be 1 (since \(2 - 1 = 1\), which is a positive integer). Therefore, \((2, 1)\) is a pair.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - If \(a = 3\), then \(b\) can be 1, 5, 7, or 9 (since \(3 - 1 = 2\), \(3 - 5 = 2\), \(3 - 7 = 4\), and \(3 - 9 = 6\), which are all positive integers).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - If \(a = 4\), then \(b\) can be 1, 5, or 9 (since \(4 - 1 = 3\), \(4 - 5 = 1\), and \(4 - 9 = -5\), which are all positive integers but not all distinct pairs).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - If \(a = 5\), then \(b\) can be 1, 7, or 9 (since \(5 - 1 = 4\), \(5 - 7 = 2\), and \(5 - 9 = -4\), which are all positive integers but not all distinct pairs).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - If \(a = 6\), then \(b\) can be 1 or 13 (since \(6 - 1 = 5\), \(6 - 13 = -7\), which are all positive integers but not all distinct pairs).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - If \(a = 7\), then \(b\) can be 1, 5, or 11 (since \(7 - 1 = 6\), \(7 - 5 = 2\), and \(7 - 11 = -4\), which are all positive integers but not all distinct pairs).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - If \(a = 8\), then \(b\) can be 1, 5, 9, or 11 (since \(8 - 1 = 7\), \(8 - 5 = 3\), \(8 - 9 = -1\), and \(8 - 11 = -3\), which are all positive integers but not all distinct pairs).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - If \(a = 9\), then \(b\) can be 1, 5, or 13 (since \(9 - 1 = 8\), \(9 - 5 = 4\), and \(9 - 13 = -4\), which are all positive integers but not all distinct pairs).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - If \(a = 10\), then \(b\) can be 1, 5, or 11 (since \(10 - 1 = 9\), \(10 - 5 = 5\), and \(10 - 11 = -1\), which are all positive integers but not all distinct pairs).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - If \(a = 11\), then \(b\) can be 1, 7, or 13 (since \(11 - 1 = 10\), \(11 - 7 = 4\), and \(11 - 13 = 2\), which are all positive integers but not all distinct pairs).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - If \(a = 12\), then \(b\) can be 1, 7, or 19 (since \(12 - 1 = 11\), \(12 - 7 = 5\), and \(12 - 19 = -7\), which are all positive integers but not all distinct pairs).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - If \(a = 13\), then \(b\) can be 1, 5, or 19 (since \(13 - 1 = 12\), \(13 - 5 = 8\), and \(13 - 19 = -6\), which are all positive integers but not all distinct pairs).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, we count the distinct pairs \((2, 1), (3, 1), (3, 5), (3, 7), (3, 9), (3, 11), (4, 1), (4, 5), (4, 9), (4, 13), (5, 1), (5, 7), (5, 9), (5, 11), (5, 13), (6, 1), (6, 5), (6, 9), (6, 11), (6, 13), (7, 1), (7, 5), (7, 9), (7, 11), (7, 13), (8, 1), (8, 5), (8, 9), (8, 11), (8, 13), (9, 1), (9, 5), (9, 7), (9, 11), (9, 13), (10, 1), (10, 5), (10, 9), (10, 11), (10, 13), (11, 1), (11, 7), (11, 9), (11, 13), (12, 1), (12, 5), (12, 9), (12, 11), (12, 13), (13, 1), (13, 5), (13, 9), (13, 11), (13, 13)\). After removing duplicates, we have 25 distinct pairs.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the number of distinct positive integers that can be represented as the difference of two numbers in the set \(\{1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13\}\) is \(\boxed{25}\).<|endoftext|>
