[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Two lines are perpendicular.  One line has a direction vector of $\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -7 \end{pmatrix}.$  The other line has a direction vector of $\begin{pmatrix} a \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}.$  Find $a.$
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Given that two lines are perpendicular, their direction vectors must be orthogonal vectors. Therefore, one of the direction vectors must be a multiple of the other direction vector.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The direction vectors are given as:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. $\mathbf{D_1} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -7 \end{pmatrix}$ 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. $\mathbf{D_2} = \begin{pmatrix} a \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}$
