[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To factor the expression \( t^2 - 121 \), we can recognize that it is a difference of squares. The general form of a difference of squares is \( a^2 - b^2 \), which factors into \( (a - b)(a + b) \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m In the expression \( t^2 - 121 \), we can identify \( a = t \) and \( b = 11 \), because \( 121 = 11^2 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Using the difference of squares formula \( (a - b)(a + b) \), we substitute \( a \) and \( b \) with \( t \) and \( 11 \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m t^2 - 121 = (t - 11)(t + 11)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the factored form of \( t^2 - 121 \) is \(\boxed{(t - 11)(t + 11)}\).<|endoftext|>
