[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To solve for \( n \) in the range \(-90 \le n \le 90\) such that \(\sin n^\circ = \cos 456^\circ\), we start by using the fact that \(\cos x = \sin(x - 90^\circ)\). Therefore, we can rewrite the angle \(456^\circ\) in terms of a relevant angle within one full rotation.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, we calculate \(456^\circ - 90^\circ\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 456^\circ - 90^\circ = 366^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, we need to find \( n \) such that:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \sin n^\circ = \cos 366^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since \(\cos 366^\circ = \cos (366^\circ - 360^\circ) = \cos 6^\circ\), we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \sin n^\circ = \cos 6^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The angle \(6^\circ\) is within the range of \(-90^\circ\) to \(90^\circ\), so \(\sin n^\circ = \cos 6^\circ\) is satisfied for any angle \( n \). Therefore, any angle \( n \) in the range \(-90^\circ \le n \le 90^\circ\) will satisfy the equation.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The final answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{(-90, 90)}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
