[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To convert the point \((1, -1, -6)\) from rectangular coordinates \((x, y, z)\) to cylindrical coordinates \((r, \theta, z)\), we need to use the following relationships:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ x = r \cos(\theta) \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ y = r \sin(\theta) \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ z = z \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m where \( z \) is the height of the object in the \( z \)-direction, \( r \) is the distance from the surface to the \( xy \)-plane in the \( z\)-direction, and \( \theta \) is the angle between the positive \( z \)-axis and the line components of the position vector.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Given the point \((1, -1, -6)\), we can identify:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ r = 1 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \theta = \pi \] (since the point is in the \( xy \)-plane)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ z = -6 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, we can calculate the values of \( r \) and \( \theta \) using the formulas:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ r = \sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \theta = \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{y}{x}\right) \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ z = z \]
