[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the diameter of a circle whose area is \(100\pi \text{cm}^2\), we will follow these steps:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. **Recall the formula for the area of a circle:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    A = \pi r^2
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    where \(A\) is the area and \(r\) is the radius.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. **Set the area equal to \(100\pi \text{cm}^2\):**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \pi r^2 = 100\pi
