[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m One computer in a lab is programmed to back up data at the turn of the minute every five minutes. Another computer is programmed to back up data at the turn of the minute every two minutes. Find the number of times in twenty-four hours that the two computers back up data at the same time.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m (Assume that the computers do not back up at the start of the 24-hour period.)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the number of times both computers back up data at the same time in 24 hours, we need to find pairs of times when both computers are running simultaneously within the 24-hour period. Each of these pairs corresponds to one occurrence of the computers backing up data at the same time.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, let's break down the times when one computer backs up data at the turn of the minute every five minutes:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - From 10:00 AM to 10:59:59
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - From 11:00 AM to 11:59:59
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - And so on...
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The general formula for the \(n\)-th minute when one computer back ups data is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Time sequence} = 10 + \left(\frac{5(n-1)}{5}\right) \text{ minutes to the next load}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Simplifying this, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Time sequence} = 10 + (n - 1) \text{ minutes}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, let's break down the times when the other computer backs up data at the turn of the minute every two minutes:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - From 10:00 AM to 10:29:59
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - From 10:30 AM to 10:59:59
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - And so on...
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The general formula for the \(n\)-th minute when the other computer backs ups data is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Time sequence} = 10 + \left(\frac{2(n-1)}{2}\right) \text{ minutes to the next load}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Simplifying this, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Time sequence} = 10 + (n - 1) \text{ minutes}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We need to find the number of pairs of consecutive minutes during which the two sequences overlap. This means we need \(n\) such that:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m (n-1) \leq 5k \quad \text{and} \quad (n-1) \leq 2m \quad \text{for some integer } k \text{ and } m
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m which simplifies to:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m k \leq \frac{n-1}{5} \quad \text{and} \quad m \leq \frac{n-1}{2}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Let's find the maximum possible \(n\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m n = 24 \times 10.5 = 246
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, there are 246 minutes in 24 hours that one computer backs up data at the turn of the minute every five minutes.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, let's find the maximum possible \(m\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m m = 246 \times 1.5 = 369
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, there are 369 minutes in 24 hours that the other computer backs up data at the turn of the minute every two minutes.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We need to count the number of pairs \((k, m)\) such that \(k\) and \(m\) are integers and \(k \leq \frac{n-1}{5}\) and \(m \leq \frac{n-1}{2}\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
