[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Suppose that I have an unlimited supply of identical math books, history books, and physics books.  All are the same size, and I have room on a shelf for 8 books.  In how many ways can I arrange eight books on the shelf if no two books of the same type can be adjacent?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To solve this problem, we need to ensure that no two books of the same type are adjacent when arranged on the shelf. We have three types of books: math books, history books, and physics books, all of the same size.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Here’s the step-by-step reasoning:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. **Determine the number of choices for each position:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - For the first book, there are 3 possible types (math, history, or physics).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - For the second book, there are 2 possible types (since one type must be at the start and one at the end).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - For the third book, there is 1 possible type (since one initially and one at the end).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - For the fourth book, there is 1 possible type (since three types must cover the fourth position).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - For the fifth book, there is 1 possible type (since four types must cover the fifth position).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - For the sixth book, there is 1 possible type (since five types must cover the sixth position).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - And so on...
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. **Calculate the total number of arrangements:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The total number of ways to arrange 8 books where no two of the same kind are adjacent is the product of the number of choices at each position.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The formula for the number of valid arrangements is \(3 \times 2 \times 1 \times 1 \times 1 \times 1 \times 1 \times 1 = 3!\) (since we are arranging 8 positions).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Calculating \(3!\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3! = 3 \times 2 \times 1 = 6
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, the total number of ways to arrange 8 books on the shelf is \(\boxed{6}\).<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
