[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m What is the period of $y = \tan x + \cot x$?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the period of the function \( y = \tan x + \cot x \), we need to determine the values of \( \theta \) such that \( \tan(\theta + k\pi) = \tan x \) for any integer \( k \). 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Let's start by examining the functions \( \tan x \) and \( \cot x \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. The function \( \tan x \) has a period of \( \pi \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \tan(x + \pi) = \tan x
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. The function \( \cot x \) has a period of \( \pi \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \cot(x + \pi) = \cot x
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, we need to find a common period that satisfies:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \tan(\theta + k\pi) = \tan x
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This implies:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \theta + k\pi = x + n\pi
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m for any integer \( n \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find a common period, we set the arguments equal to each other:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \theta + k\pi = x + (m+1)\pi \quad \text{for some integers } m \text{ and } n
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Solving for \( \theta \), we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \theta = (x + m\pi) - k\pi
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The period \( T \) of the function \( y = \tan x + \cot x \) can be found by finding the largest interval within which this period occurs. Since \( \tan x \) and \( \cot x \) have the same period, the period of \( y = \tan x + \cot x \) is also \( \pi \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the period of \( y = \tan x + \cot x \) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{\pi}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \pi
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m [Is Boxed Matched]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m True
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
