[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m How many orbitals can fill completely in an atom if all subshells have one orbital?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine how many orbitals can fill completely in an atom if all subshells have one orbital, we need to consider the properties of subshells in an atom.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. **Subshells and Subshells:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - A subshell is a set of atomic orbitals that are occupied by electrons from a given principal quantum number \( n \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The three subshells are \( n = 1, 2, \) and \( 3 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - Each subshell contains \( n+1 \) orbitals, and within each subshell, there is only one orbital.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. **Fillability:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The filling of subshells follows the Aufbau principle, which states that electrons fill the lowest energy orbitals first.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - In the case of only one orbital from each subshell, there is no possibility of filling the higher orbitals consecutively.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3. **Conclusion:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - Since each subshell has only one orbital and there is no possibility of filling the higher orbitals consecutively, all orbitals in an atom can be completely filled with one orbital.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the number of orbitals that can fill completely in an atom is \(\boxed{1}\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
