[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find \(\tan \frac{9 \pi}{4}\), we start by simplifying the angle \(\frac{9 \pi}{4}\). We recognize that \(\frac{9 \pi}{4} = 2\pi + \frac{\pi}{4}\), because \(\frac{\pi}{4}\) is exactly half of a complete rotation. This means that \(\frac{9 \pi}{4}\) is the same as going around the circle 9 times and then starting at the same position as we were at the start of the 9th revolution. Therefore, the angle \(\frac{9 \pi}{4}\) is equivalent to having traveled an additional \(\pi\) radians. We can express this as:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \frac{9 \pi}{4} = 2\pi + \frac{\pi}{4}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The tangent of an angle is the ratio of the sine of that angle to the cosine of that angle. So, we can write:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \tan \left( \frac{9 \pi}{4} \right) = \tan \left( 2\pi + \frac{\pi}{4} \right) = \tan \left( \frac{\pi}{4} \right)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We know from trigonometric values that \(\tan \frac{\pi}{4} = 1\). Therefore, the value of \(\tan \frac{9 \pi}{4}\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{1}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
