[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Six horses eat 12 bales of hay in 12 hours. At the same rate, how many hours will 36 bales of hay last 12 horses?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine how many hours 36 bales of hay will last for 12 horses, we start by finding the rate at which the horses eat hay. 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, we know that six horses eat 12 bales of hay in 12 hours. Therefore, the number of bales the horses eat per hour is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \frac{12 \text{ bales}}{12 \text{ hours}} = 1 \text{ bale per hour}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This means that each horse eats 1 bale of hay per hour.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we need to find out how many hours 36 bale of hay will last for 12 horses. Since each horse eats 1 bale per hour, in 1 hour, 12 horses will eat:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 12 \text{ horses/hour}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the number of hours 36 bales of hay will last for 12 horses is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \frac{36 \text{ bales}}{12 \text{ bales per hour}} = 3 \text{ hours}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
