[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Anna, Stephanie, and James all start running around a track at 8:00. Anna completes a lap every 4 minutes, Stephanie finishes a lap every 7 minutes, and James finishes a lap every 6 minutes. What is the earliest time when all three meet back at the beginning?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Well, let's gather the information we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - Anna's lap rate: Anna completes 1 lap every 4 minutes.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - Stephanie's lap rate: Stephanie completes 1 lap every 7 minutes.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - James's lap rate: James completes 1 lap every 6 minutes.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We want to find the earliest time when all three meet back at the beginning of the track.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since each person completes a lap at their own unique rate, we should find the least common multiple (LCM) of their lap lengths to see when they will all complete a lap together.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Let's calculate the LCM of 4, 7, and 6.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The prime factorization of the numbers is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - 4: 2^2
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - 7: 7 (it's a prime number itself)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - 6: 2 × 3
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The LCM is thus the product of all the prime factors with the highest powers:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \text{LCM} = 2^2 × 3 × 7 = 4 × 3 × 7 = 84 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This means that they will all complete a 4-minute, 7-minute, or 6-minute lap together. The earliest time when all three meet back at the beginning would be:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - Anna's lap: 84 minutes (since Anna starts at 8:00 and completes every 4 minutes)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - Stephanie's lap: 84 minutes (since Stephanie starts at 8:00 and completes every 7 minutes)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - James's lap: 84 minutes (since James starts at 8:00 and completes every 6 minutes)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, the earliest time when Anna, Stephanie, and James meet back at the beginning is 8:00 plus 4*7 = 28 minutes.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m If we convert this back to time format, it's 8:28.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, the earliest time when all three meet back at the beginning is \boxed{8:28}.<|endoftext|>
