[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m How many ordered pairs of real numbers $(x,y)$ satisfy the following system of equations? \[\left\{ \begin{aligned} x+3y&=3 \\ \left| |x| - |y| \right| &= 1 \end{aligned}\right.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To solve the system of equations given by
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \left\{ \begin{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m x+3y&=3 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \left| |x| - |y| \right| &= 1
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \end{aligned}\right.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m we will break it down step by step.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ### Step 1: Solve the first equation for \(x\)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The first equation is
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m x + 3y = 3
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We can solve for \(x\) by isolating it on one side:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m x = 3 - 3y
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ### Step 2: Analyze the second equation
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The second equation is
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \left| |x| - |y| \right| = 1
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This absolute value equation can be split into two cases:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. \(|x| - |y| = 1\)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. \(|x| - |y| = -1\)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ### Step 3: Solve the second equation for \(x\) and \(y\)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m #### Case 1: \(|x| - |y| = 1\)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m If \(|x| - |y| = 1\), we have two sub-cases:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \(x - y = 1\)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \(y - x = 1\)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m #### Case 2: \(|x| - |y| = -1\)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m If \(|x| - |y| = -1\), we have two sub-cases:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \(x - y = -1\)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \(y - x = -1\)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ### Step 4: Solve the third equation
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The third equation is
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m x + 3y = 3
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We can solve for \(x\) again by isolating it on one side:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m x = 3 - 3y
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ### Step 5: Determine the number of valid ordered pairs
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We need to ensure that the solutions for \(x\) and \(y\) meet both original equations. 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m #### Case 1: \(x - y = 1\)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We have \(x = 3 - 3y\). Substitute \(x\) into the second equation \(x + 3y = 3\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m (3 - 3y) + 3y = 3
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This simplifies to:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3 = 3
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This is always true, so all valid solutions for \(y\) are in this case.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m #### Case 2: \(x - y = -1\)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Similarly, we have \(x = 3 + 3y\). Substitute \(x\) into the second equation \(x + 3y = 3\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m (3 + 3y) + 3y = 3
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This simplifies to:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3 = 3
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Again, this is always true, so all valid solutions for \(y\) are in this case.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ### Step 6: Check the domain constraints
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Both sub-cases \(x = 3 - 3y\) and \(x = 3 + 3y\) must satisfy \(0 \leq x \leq 3\). This means:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 0 \leq 3 - 3y \leq 3 \quad \text{and} \quad 0 \leq 3 + 3y \leq 3
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Simplifying these inequalities:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m -3 \leq -3y \leq 0 \quad \Rightarrow \quad 0 \leq y \leq 1
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3 \leq 3y \leq 3 \quad \Rightarrow \quad 1 \leq y \leq 1
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Both inequalities hold for \(y\) in the interval \([0, 1]\). However, \(y\) cannot also be negative because then \(x = 3 - 3y\) would be negative and the absolute value equation is not valid.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ### Conclusion
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, all valid pairs \((x, y)\) are within the interval \([0, 1]\). Since any point on the line \(x = 3 - 3y\) and the vice versa satisfies the system, and there are infinitely many points in each segment, we conclude there are infinitely many ordered pairs.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the final answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{\infty}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
