[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Find the area of the region enclosed by the graph of $|x-60|+|y|=\left|\frac{x}{4}\right|.$
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the area of the region enclosed by the graph of \( |x-60| + |y| = \left| \frac{x}{4} \right| \), we need to analyze the equation step by step.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. **Simplify the Absolute Value Expressions:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    The equation \( |x-60| + |y| = \left| \frac{x}{4} \right| \) can be split into separate cases based on the value of \( \frac{x}{4} \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    |x-60| + |y| = \begin{cases}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    x-60 + y & \text{if } \frac{x}{4} \geq 0 \text{ and } \frac{y}{4} \geq 0 \implies x+4y-60 \geq 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    60 - x + y & \text{if } \frac{x}{4} \geq 0 \text{ and } \frac{y}{4} \leq 0 \implies x-4y+60 \geq 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    x-60 + y & \text{if } \frac{x}{4} \leq 0 \text{ and } \frac{y}{4} \geq 0 \implies -x+4y-60 \geq 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    60 - x + y & \text{if } \frac{x}{4} \leq 0 \text{ and } \frac{y}{4} \leq 0 \implies -60 - y + 4x \geq 0
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \end{cases}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    Simplifying each case:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    -(x-60) + |y| = \begin{cases}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    x-60 + y & \text{if } x+4y-60 \geq 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    -x+4y+60 & \text{if } -x+4y+60 \geq 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    -x+60 + y & \text{if } -60 - y + 4x \geq 0
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \end{cases}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    or
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    x-60 + |y| = \begin{cases}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    -x+60 + y & \text{if } -60 - y + 4x \geq 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    x+4y-60 & \text{if } x-4y+60 \geq 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    -x-60 + y & \text{if } -60 - y + 4x \geq 0
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \end{cases}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. **Graphical Interpretation:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    The equation \( |x-60| + |y| = \left| \frac{x}{4} \right| \) represents a region in the \( xy \)-plane bounded by the lines \( x-60 = \frac{x}{4} \), \( y = \frac{x}{4} \), \( x-60 = \left| - \frac{x}{4} \right| \), and \( y = \left| - \frac{x}{4} \right| \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3. **Finding the Area:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The line \( x-60 = \frac{x}{4} \) intersects the lines \( y = \frac{x}{4} \) and cuts the square at \( x = 60 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The line \( y = \frac{x}{4} \) intersects the lines \( x-60 = \left| - \frac{x}{4} \right| \) and \( x-60 = \left| - \frac{x}{4} \right| \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The line \( x-60 = \left| - \frac{x}{4} \right| \) is symmetric about the x-axis.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The line \( y = \left| - \frac{x}{4} \right| \) is also symmetric about the x-axis.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    To find the exact area, we integrate over the region defined by these lines.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 4. **Integration Bounds:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The region is bounded by \( y = \frac{x}{4} \), \( y = \frac{x}{4} \), \( x-60 = \frac{x}{4} \), and \( x-60 = \left| - \frac{x}{4} \right| \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - Solving \( x-60 = \frac{x}{4} \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      x-60 = \frac{x}{4} \implies 4x-240 = x \implies 3x = 240 \implies x = 80
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      So, the line \( y = \frac{x}{4} \) intersects \( x = 80 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The region is bounded by \( y = \frac{x}{4} \) and \( x-60 = \left| - \frac{x}{4} \right| \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      - For \( y = \frac{x}{4} \), the line \( x-60 = \frac{x}{4} \) intersects the line \( x+y = 120 \) at \( (120, 80) \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      - For \( x-60 = \left| - \frac{x}{4} \right| \), the region is symmetric about the x-axis, so the intersection is at \( (120, 80) \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since we only need the region bounded by these lines, the area is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{800}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
