[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the other endpoint of the line segment given the midpoint and one endpoint, we can use the midpoint formula in reverse. The midpoint \( M \) of a line segment with endpoints \( A(x_1, y_1) \) and \( B(x_2, y_2) \) is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m M = \left( \frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \right)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Given:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - The midpoint \( M \) is \((3, -2)\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - One endpoint is \((1, 6)\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Let the unknown endpoint be \((x, y)\). According to the formula for the midpoint, we can set up the following equations:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
