[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the smallest positive integer divisible by \(10\), \(11\), and \(12\), we need to find the least common multiple (LCM) of these three numbers. We will use the prime factorizations of each number to find the LCM.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. **Prime factorization of each number:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - \(10 = 2 \times 5\)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - \(11 = 11\)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - \(12 = 2^2 \times 3\)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. **Determine the highest power of each prime that appears in the factorizations:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The highest power of \(2\) is \(2^2\) (from \(12\)).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The highest power of \(3\) is \(3^1\) (from \(3\)).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The highest power of \(5\) is \(5^1\) (from \(10\)).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The highest power of \(11\) is \(11^1\) (from \(11\)).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3. **Calculate the LCM by taking the product of these highest powers:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \text{LCM} = 2^2 \times 3^1 \times 5^1 \times 11^1
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 4. **Compute the value:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    2^2 = 4
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    3^1 = 3
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    5^1 = 5
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    11^1 = 11
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \text{LCM} = 4 \times 3 \times 5 \times 11
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 5. **Multiply these values step by step:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    4 \times 3 = 12
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    12 \times 5 = 60
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    60 \times 11 = 660
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the smallest positive integer divisible by \(10\), \(11\), and \(12\) is \(\boxed{660}\).<|endoftext|>
