[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the value of \( k \) in the line equation \( -x + y = k \) that passes through the points \((-1, 6)\), \((6, k)\), and \((20, 3)\), we need to ensure that these points satisfy the equation. This means we can set up three equations using the coordinates of the points.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. The first point is \((-1, 6)\), so substitute \((-1, 6)\) and \((-1, 6)\) into the equation:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    -(-1) + 6 = k \implies 1 + 6 = k \implies k = 7
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. The second point is \((6, k)\), so substitute \((6, k)\) and \((6, 7)\) into the equation:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    -6 + 7 = k \implies 1 = k
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3. The third point is \((20, 3)\), so substitute \((20, 3)\) and \((20, 7)\) into the equation:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    -20 + 7 = k \implies -13 = k
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We see that the value of \( k \) is consistent across all three points. Therefore, the value of \( k \) is \(\boxed{1}\).<|endoftext|>
