[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m A point $P$ is chosen at random in the interior of equilateral triangle $ABC$. What is the probability that $\triangle ABP$ has a greater area than each of $\triangle ACP$ and $\triangle BCP$?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the probability that \(\triangle ABP\) has a greater area than each of \(\triangle ACP\) and \(\triangle BCP\), we start by noting that \(\triangle ABP\) is composed of three small triangles: \(\triangle ACP\), \(\triangle BCP\), and \(\triangle APO\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, we calculate the area of \(\triangle ABP\). Note that \(\triangle APO\) is similar to \(\triangle ABC\) and has the same height as \(\triangle ABC\). The area of \(\triangle APO\) is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Area of } \triangle APO = \frac{1}{2} \times AP \times PO \times \sin(\angle AOP)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since \(P\) is chosen randomly within \(\triangle ABC\), the probability that \(\triangle APO\) lies entirely within \(\triangle ABC\) is \(\frac{1}{2}\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we consider the possible areas of \(\triangle ABP\) that are greater than \(\triangle ACP\) and \(\triangle BCP\). For \(\triangle ABP\) to have a greater area than \(\triangle ACP\), the area of the remaining triangle \(\triangle APB\) must be greater than the area of \(\triangle ACP\), which is \(\frac{1}{4}\) of the area of \(\triangle ABC\). Similarly, for \(\triangle ABP\) to have a greater area than \(\triangle BCP\), the area of the remaining triangle \(\triangle BPA\) must be greater than the area of \(\triangle BCP\), which is \(\frac{1}{4}\) of the area of \(\triangle ABC\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The total area of \(\triangle ABC\) is \(\frac{1}{2}\) of the area of the largest possible equilateral triangle, which is \(\frac{1}{12}\) of the area of the cube. Therefore, the area of \(\triangle ABP\) that is greater than \(\triangle ACP\), \(\triangle BCP\), and potentially more than both, is a smaller part of the total area. By symmetry and the properties of similar triangles, the probability that \(\triangle ABP\) has a greater area than \(\triangle ACP\), \(\triangle BCP\), and potentially more than both is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2 \times \left(1 - \frac{1}{4}\right)^3 = 2 \times \left(\frac{3}{4}\right)^3 = 2 \times \frac{27}{64} = \frac{54}{64} = \frac{27}{32}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the probability that \(\triangle ABP\) has a greater area than each of \(\triangle ACP\) and \(\triangle BCP\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{\frac{27}{32}}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \frac{1}{3}
