[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m A rectangular tile measures 3 inches by 4 inches. What is the fewest number of these tiles that are needed to completely cover a rectangular region that is 2 feet by 5 feet?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the fewest number of tiles needed to cover a rectangular region, we need to determine how many tiles fit along each dimension of the region.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, we convert the dimensions of the region to inches:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - The region is 2 feet by 5 feet.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - 1 foot is equal to 12 inches.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, the dimensions in inches are:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - Length: \(2 \text{ feet} \times 12 \text{ inches/foot} = 24 \text{ inches}\)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - Width: \(5 \text{ feet} \times 12 \text{ inches/foot} = 60 \text{ inches}\)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Each tile measures 3 inches by 4 inches. We need to determine how many tiles fit along the length and the width of the region.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. Along the length:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \frac{24 \text{ inches}}{3 \text{ inches/tile}} = 8 \text{ tiles}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. Along the width:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \frac{60 \text{ inches}}{4 \text{ inches/tile}} = 15 \text{ tiles}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Adding these together gives the total number of tiles needed:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 8 \text{ tiles} + 15 \text{ tiles} = 23 \text{ tiles}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the fewest number of tiles needed to completely cover the rectangular region is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{23}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 120
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m [Is Boxed Matched]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m True
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
