[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m How many natural-number factors does $\textit{N}$ have if $\textit{N} = 2^3 \cdot 3^2 \cdot 5^1$?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the number of natural-number factors of \( N = 2^3 \cdot 3^2 \cdot 5^1 \), we need to use the formula for finding the total number of factors of a number based on its prime factorization.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m An integer \( N \) with a prime factorization of the form \( N = p_1^{e_1} \cdot p_2^{e_2} \cdot \ldots \cdot p_k^{e_k} \) has a total of \( (e_1 + 1)(e_2 + 1) \cdots (e_k + 1) \) factors. This is because each prime power can be factored into some part of the product.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m For the given \( N = 2^3 \cdot 3^2 \cdot 5^1 \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - The exponent of 2 is 3, so it can be factored into \( e_1 = 3 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - The exponent of 3 is 2, so it can be factored into \( e_2 = 2 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - The exponent of 5 is 1, so it can be factored into \( e_3 = 1 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, we calculate the total number of factors:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m (e_1 + 1)(e_2 + 1)(e_3 + 1) = (3 + 1)(2 + 1)(1 + 1) = 4 \cdot 3 \cdot 2 = 24
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the number of natural-number factors of \( N \) is \(\boxed{24}\).<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
