[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the tens digit of \(5^{2005}\), we need to find the remainder when \(5^{2005}\) is divided by 100. This is equivalent to finding \(5^{2005} \mod 100\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, we use Euler's theorem, which states that if \(a\) and \(n\) are coprime, then \(a^{\phi(n)} \equiv 1 \pmod{n}\), where \(\phi\) is the Euler's totient function. For \(n = 100\), \(\phi(100) = 100 \left(1 - \frac{1}{2}\right)\left(1 - \frac{1}{5}\right) = 40\). Since 5 and 100 are coprime, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[5^{40} \equiv 1 \pmod{100}\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Using this, we can reduce the exponent 2005 modulo 40:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[2005 \div 40 = 50 \text{ remainder } 5 \Rightarrow 2005 \equiv 5 \pmod{40}\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[5^{2005} \equiv 5^5 \pmod{100}\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, we calculate \(5^5\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[5^5 = 5 \times 5 \times 5 \times 5 \times 5 = 3125\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we find the remainder when 3125 is divided by 100:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[3125 \div 100 = 31 \text{ remainder } 25 \Rightarrow 3125 \equiv 25 \pmod{100}\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the tens digit of \(5^{2005}\) is \(\boxed{25}\).<|endoftext|>
