[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the tens digit of \( 5^{2005} \), we need to determine \( 5^{2005} \mod 100 \). This is because the tens digit of a number is influenced by its last two digits.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, observe that \( 5^3 = 125 \), which has a last two digits of \( 25 \). Since 25 is divisible by 100, we know that \( 5^{2005} \) will have at least two "2's" in its last two digits. Therefore, we only need to look at the last two digits of \( 5^{2005} \), which is the same as looking at \( 5^{2005} \mod 100 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We can use properties of modular arithmetic to simplify this. Notice that:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 100 = 99 \cdot 1 + 1 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This tells us that the last two digits of \( 5^{2005} \) are the same as the last two digits of \( 5^1 \). Therefore, we need to find \( 5^1 \mod 100 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Calculating \( 5^1 \mod 100 \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 5^1 = 5 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the last two digits of \( 5^{2005} \) are 05. The tens digit of 05 is 5.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the tens digit of \( 5^{2005} \) is \(\boxed{5}\).<|endoftext|>
