[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Javier is going to Disneyland during spring break. He plans on visiting four particular attractions all before lunch. In how many orders could he visit all four attractions once?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the number of different orders Javier can visit all four attractions during his trip, we need to calculate the number of permutations of 4 attractions taken all at once, which is denoted as \( P(4, 4) \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The formula for the number of permutations of \( n \) items taken \( r \) at a time is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m P(n, r) = \frac{n!}{(n-r)!}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m In this problem, \( n = 4 \) (since Javier is visiting four attractions) and \( r = 4 \) (since he wants to visit each of the four attractions exactly once). Plugging these values into the formula, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m P(4, 4) = \frac{4!}{(4-4)!} = \frac{4!}{0!}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since \( 0! = 1 \), the formula simplifies to:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m P(4, 4) = \frac{4!}{1} = 4!
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Calculating \( 4! \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 4! = 4 \times 3 \times 2 \times 1 = 24
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the number of different orders Javier can visit all four attractions once is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{24}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
