[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Javier is going to Disneyland during spring break. He plans on visiting four particular attractions all before lunch. In how many orders could he visit all four attractions once?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the number of ways Javier can visit four specific attractions during the spring break, we need to consider the permutations of the attractions themselves. Since there are four attractions (A, B, C, D) and Javier visits each attraction exactly once, we need to calculate the number of permutations of the four attractions.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The number of permutations of \( n \) items where some items are identical is given by \( P(n, n) = n! \) (n factorial), which is the product of all positive integers up to \( n \). For four attractions, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m P(4, 4) = 4! = 4 \times 3 \times 2 \times 1 = 24
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the number of ways Javier can visit all four attractions once is \(\boxed{24}\).<|endoftext|>
