[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m A quadrilateral in the plane has vertices $(1, 3)$, $(1, 1)$, $(2, 1)$ and $(2006, 2007)$. How many square units is the area of the quadrilateral?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the area of the quadrilateral with vertices \((1, 3)\), \((1, 1)\), \((2, 1)\), and \((2006, 2007)\), we can use the Shoelace Theorem (also known as Gauss's area formula). The Shoelace Theorem states that for a polygon with vertices \((x_1, y_1), (x_2, y_2), \ldots, (x_n, y_n)\), the area \(A\) is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m A = \frac{1}{2} \left| \sum_{i=1}^{n-1} (x_i y_{i+1} - y_i x_{i+1}) + (x_n y_1 - y_n x_1) \right|
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m For the quadrilateral with vertices \((1, 3)\), \((1, 1)\), \((2, 1)\), and \((2006, 2007)\), we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m x_1 = 1, \quad y_1 = 3, \quad x_2 = 1, \quad y_2 = 1, \quad x_3 = 2, \quad y_3 = 1, \quad x_4 = 2006, \quad y_4 = 2007
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The first step is to sum the products of the coordinates taken two at a time:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \begin{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \sum_{i=1}^{4} (x_i y_{i+1}) &= (1 \cdot 1) + (1 \cdot 1) + (2 \cdot 1) + (2006 \cdot 1) \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m &= 1 + 1 + 2 + 2006 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m &= 2020
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \end{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \begin{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \sum_{i=1}^{4} (y_i x_{i+1}) &= (3 \cdot 1) + (1 \cdot 1) + (1 \cdot 2) + (2007 \cdot 2006) \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m &= 3 + 1 + 2 + 2007 \cdot 2006 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m &= 3 + 2007 + 4010262 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m &= 4012092
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \end{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \begin{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \sum_{i=1}^{4} (x_i y_{i+1} - y_i x_{i+1}) &= (1 \cdot 1) + (1 \cdot 1) + (2 \cdot 2) + (2006 \cdot 2007) \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m &= 1 + 1 + 4 + 2006 \cdot 2007 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m &= 5 + 2007 \cdot 4 \cdot 2006 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m &= 5 + 8034 \cdot 2006 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m &= 5 + 1614234 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m &= 1614389
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \end{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, we can apply the Shoelace Theorem:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \begin{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m A &= \frac{1}{2} \left| 2020 \cdot 1614234 - 1614389 \cdot 1 \right| \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m &= \frac{1}{2} \left| 330816760 - 1614389 \right| \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m &= \frac{1}{2} \left| 330816760 - 1614389 \right| \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m &= \frac{1}{2} \left| 330656260 \right| \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m &= \frac{1}{2} \cdot 330656260 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m &= 165328130
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \end{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the area of the quadrilateral is \(\boxed{165328130}\) square units.<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
