[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the length of \( CE \) in the given triangle \( \triangle ABE \), we start by noting that \( \triangle ABE \) and \( \triangle BCE \) are right-angled at \( E \). This means that both \( \triangle ABE \) and \( \triangle BCE \) are isosceles right triangles, because each of the right angles is either at \( A \), \( B \), or \( C \) but not at \( E \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Let's denote the length of \( BE \) and \( EC \) by \( x \). Since \( \triangle ABE \) is isosceles right, we have \( AE = EB = 24 \). The horizontal distance between \( A \) and \( B \) is \( AB = EC \), so \( AB = x \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, consider the right triangle \( \triangle BCE \). Since \( \angle BEC = 60^\circ \) and \( \angle CED = 60^\circ \), \( \triangle BCE \) is also isosceles right. This means that the horizontal side \( CE \) is equal to the hypotenuse \( BE \) because \( \angle BEC = 60^\circ \), so \( EC = BE \), which is consistent with our previous results since \( AB = BE \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, we have \( AE = PE = 24 \), and since \( E \) is directly above \( D \) and across the hypotenuse from \( B \), \( DE \) is the remainder of the base \( ED \) when considering \( E \) across the two legs of the isosceles right triangle \( BCE \). Since \( DE = BE - EB \) and \( BE + EB = 4 \times BE = 4 \times 24 = 96 \), and since \( BE = CE \), we have \( CE = BE \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the length of \( CE \) is:
