[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the number of zeros in the expansion of \( 99,\!999,\!999^2 \), we need to consider the number of times the digits '0' appear in this expansion. The expansion of \( 99,\!999,\!999^2 \) is a six-digit number consisting of \( 900,000 \) zeros, followed by \( 99,000,000 \).
