[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the perimeter of the shaded region, we will follow these steps:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. Determine the lengths of all sides of the circle.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. Calculate the perimeter of the square (which is the inner circle).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3. Subtract the length of the diameter of the circle from the perimeter of the square to get the length of the shaded region.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, let's determine the length of the circle. The radius \(OP = OQ = 5\), so each chord \(OP\) and \(OQ\) is a diameter of the circle. Therefore, each radius of the circle is \(5\) units. The length of the circle (the diameter) is \(5 \times 2 = 10\). This is because the radius is half the diameter, and it is given that the radius is 5. So the perimeter of the circle (the inner square) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 10 \times 2 = 20
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, let's find the perimeter of the square. The perimeter \(P_{\text{square}}\) of the square is the sum of the lengths of all four sides. Each side of the square is equal to the diameter of the circle, which is 10 units. Therefore, the perimeter of the square is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 4 \times 10 = 40
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Finally, the perimeter of the shaded region is the perimeter of the square minus the length of the diameter of the circle:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 40 - 10 = 30
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the perimeter of the shaded region is \(\boxed{30}\).<|endoftext|>
