[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To evaluate \(\log_3 81\), we need to determine the exponent to which the base 3 must be raised to obtain 81. In other words, we need to find \(x\) such that:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[3^x = 81\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We can rewrite 81 as a power of 3. We know that:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[81 = 3^4\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, we can write the equation as:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[3^x = 3^4\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since the bases are the same, we can set the exponents equal to each other:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[x = 4\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, \(\log_3 81 = 4\).
