[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the largest possible value of the sum \(I + M + O\) for distinct positive integers \(I\), \(M\), and \(O\) such that \(I \cdot M \cdot O = 2001\), we need to factorize 2001 into three distinct positive factors.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, let's list the factorization of 2001:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 2001 = 3 \times 667 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now we need to consider the factors of 2001 and find all possible sets of distinct positive integers \(I\), \(M\), and \(O\) that multiply to 2001. The factors of 2001 are:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 1, 3, 667, 2001 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We will list the factor triplets \((I, M, O)\) by taking each factor pair \((I, M)\) and ensuring \(O\) is distinct from \(I\) and \(M\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. If \(I = 1\), then \(M\) and \(O\) must be factors of 2001 that are distinct from 1. The only possible triplet is \((1, 17, 125)\) because:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[ 17 \times 125 = 2001 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. If \(I = 3\), then:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[ 3 \times M \times O = 2001 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    The possible pairs \((3, O)\) and \((M, 667)\) must be distinct and \(O\) must be distinct from 3. The possible pairs are:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[ (3, 667) \quad \text{and} \quad (667, 3) \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    Both yield the same triplet because \(667 \times 3 = 2001\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3. If \(I = 667\), then:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[ 667 \times M \times O = 2001 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    The possible pairs \((667, M)\) and \((O, 3)\) (where \(O = 3\)) must be distinct. The possible pair is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[ (667, 3) \quad \text{and} \quad (3, 667) \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    Both yield the same triplet because \(667 \times 3 = 2001\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 4. If \(I = 2001\), then:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[ 2001 \times M \times O = 2001 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    The only possible triplet is \((2001, 1, 1)\) because:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[ 2001 \times 1 \times 1 = 2001 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the three distinct triplet values are \((1, 17, 125)\), \((1, 3, 667)\), and \((667, 3, 125)\). The maximum value of the sum \(I + M + O\) is obtained when \(I = 1\), \(M = 17\), and \(O = 125\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ I + M + O = 1 + 17 + 125 = 143 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the largest possible value of the sum \(I + M + O\) is:
