[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Let $\mathbf{R}$ be the matrix for rotating about the origin counter-clockwise by an angle of $58^\circ.$  Find $\det \mathbf{R}.$
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the determinant of the rotation matrix $\mathbf{R}$, we start by recalling the general form of a rotation matrix. For a counterclockwise rotation by an angle $\theta$ around the origin, the rotation matrix is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \mathbf{R} = \begin{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \cos \theta & -\sin \theta \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \sin \theta & \cos \theta
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Given that the angle $\theta = 58^\circ$, we can substitute this value into the rotation matrix:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \mathbf{R} = \begin{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \cos 58^\circ & -\sin 58^\circ \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \sin 58^\circ & \cos 58^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we calculate the determinant of $\mathbf{R}$. The determinant of a $2 \times 2$ matrix \(\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \end{pmatrix}\) is given by \(ad - bc\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m For our matrix \(\mathbf{R}\), we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m a = \cos 58^\circ, \quad b = -\sin 58^\circ, \quad c = \sin 58^\circ, \quad d = \cos 58^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The determinant is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{det}(\mathbf{R}) = (\cos 58^\circ)(\cos 58^\circ) - (\sin 58^\circ)(\sin 58^\circ) = \cos^2 58^\circ - \sin^2 58^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Using the double-angle identity for cosine, \(\cos 2\theta = \cos^2 \theta - \sin^2 \theta\), we can rewrite the expression as:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \cos^2 58^\circ - \sin^2 58^\circ = \cos 116^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We know that 116 degrees is equivalent to \(270^\circ - 54^\circ\), so:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \cos 116^\circ = -\cos 54^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the determinant of \(\mathbf{R}\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \det(\mathbf{R}) = -\cos 54^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the final answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{-\cos 54^\circ}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m [Is Boxed Matched]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m True
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
