[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[(x - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^3 - 3) \dotsm (x^{11} - 11)(x^{12} - 12).\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the coefficient of \(x^{70}\) in the expansion of \((x - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^3 - 3) \dotsm (x^{11} - 11)(x^{12} - 12)\), we can start by looking at the highest power of \(x\) and its corresponding term in each factor. The highest power of \(x\) in the product is \(x^{12}\), coming from the last product in the list.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, we need to find the coefficient of \(x^{12}\) in the product \((x^{12} - 12)(x^{11} - 11)(x^{10} - 10)(x^9 - 9)(x^8 - 8)(x^7 - 7)(x^6 - 6)(x^5 - 5)(x^4 - 4)(x^3 - 3)(x^2 - 2)(x - 1)\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The coefficient of \(x^{12}\) will be the sum of the products of the coefficients of the corresponding powers of \(x\) in each of the factors taken together. However, since we are only interested in the coefficient of \(x^{12}\) from \(x^{12} - 12\), we can ignore the entire \(x^{12} - 12\) term for the coefficient.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the coefficient of \(x^{12}\) in the expansion is simply the coefficient of \(x^{12}\) in the product from the first factor.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The coefficient of \(x^{12}\) in the product \((x - 1)\) is \(1\), so the coefficient of \(x^{12}\) in the expansion of the entire product is \(1\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the coefficient of \(x^{70}\) is \(\boxed{1}\).<|endoftext|>
