[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the period of the function \( y = \cos \frac{x}{2} \), we need to determine the period of the trigonometric function \( \cos(ax) \), where \( a \) is a positive constant.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The period \( T \) of the function \( \cos(kx) \) is given by the formula:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m T = \frac{2\pi}{|k|}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m In this case, our function is \( \cos \frac{x}{2} \), so \( k = \frac{1}{2} \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Substituting \( k = \frac{1}{2} \) into the formula for the period, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m T = \frac{2\pi}{\frac{1}{2}} = 2\pi \times 2 = 4\pi
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the period of the function \( y = \cos \frac{x}{2} \) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{4\pi}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
