[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m From the equation \( b^2 = 9 \), we solve for \( b \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m b = \pm 3
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we substitute \( b = 3 \) into the expression for \( c \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m c^2 = a \implies 3^2 = a \implies a = 9
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, we verify that this value of \( a \) also satisfies the original condition for perfect square of a binomial. We substitute \( b = 3 \) and \( c^2 = 9 \) back into the binomial expansion:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m (bx + c)^2 = 3x^2 + 2 \cdot 3 \cdot x + 9 = 9x^2 + 6x + 9
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
