[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m What is the remainder when $2^{19}$ is divided by $7$?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the remainder when \(2^{19}\) is divided by \(7\), we can use the properties of modular arithmetic. Specifically, we will use the fact that \(2^6 \equiv 1 \pmod{7}\). This means that the powers of 2 repeat every 6 terms modulo 7.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, we express \(2^{19}\) in terms of the first 6 powers of 2:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[2^{19} = 2^6 \cdot 2^3.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we simplify \(2^3\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[2^3 = 8 = 2^3.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[2^{19} = 2^6 \cdot 8.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, we need to find the remainder of \(2^6 \cdot 8\) when divided by 7. Since \(8 \equiv 1 \pmod{7}\), we can substitute 8 with 1:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[2^6 \cdot 8 \equiv 2^6 \cdot 1 \pmod{7}.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We know that \(2^6 \equiv 1 \pmod{7}\), so:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[2^6 \cdot 1 \equiv 1 \cdot 1 \pmod{7},\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[2^6 \cdot 1 \equiv 1 \pmod{7}.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the remainder when \(2^{19}\) is divided by 7 is \(\boxed{1}\).<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2
