[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To solve the problem \(\frac{9}{10} + \frac{5}{6}\), we need to add two fractions with different denominators. The denominators are 10 and 6. The least common denominator (LCD) of 10 and 6 is 30. We will convert both fractions to have the denominator of 30.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, convert \(\frac{9}{10}\) to a fraction with a denominator of 30:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \frac{9}{10} = \frac{9 \times 3}{10 \times 3} = \frac{27}{30}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, convert \(\frac{5}{6}\) to a fraction with a denominator of 30:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \frac{5}{6} = \frac{5 \times 5}{6 \times 5} = \frac{25}{30}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, we can add the fractions:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \frac{27}{30} + \frac{25}{30} = \frac{27 + 25}{30} = \frac{52}{30}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The fraction \(\frac{52}{30}\) can be simplified by finding the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 52 and 30. The GCD of 52 and 30 is 2. We divide both the numerator and the denominator by 2:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \frac{52 \div 2}{30 \div 2} = \frac{26}{15}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since 26 and 15 have no common factors other than 1, the fraction \(\frac{26}{15}\) is in its simplest form.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{\frac{26}{15}}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
