[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the units digit of \(17^{13} - 17\) when divided by 10, we can simplify the problem by noting that the units digit of a number is the remainder when that number is divided by 10. Therefore, we need to find the remainder when \(17^{13}\) is divided by 10.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, observe the pattern in the units digits of the first few powers of 17:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \begin{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 17^1 & = 17 \quad \text{(units digit is 7)} \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 17^2 & = 289 \quad \text{(units digit is 9)} \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 17^3 & = 4913 \quad \text{(units digit is 3)} \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 17^4 & = 83521 \quad \text{(units digit is 1)} \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \end{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We can see that the units digits repeat every 4 terms: 7, 9, 3, 1. This periodicity implies that the units digit of \(17^n\) depends on the remainder when \(17-n\) is divided by 4.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The remainder when 13 is divided by 4 is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 13 \equiv 1 \pmod{4}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This means that \(17^{13} \equiv 17^1 \equiv 7 \pmod{10}\). Therefore, the remainder when \(17^{13}\) is divided by 10 is 7 (since the units digit is 7).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the units digit of \(17^{13} - 17\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 7 - 1 = 6
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The final answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{6}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
