[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine if the vectors \(\left\{ \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ k \end{pmatrix} \right\}\) are linearly dependent, we need to check if one vector is a scalar multiple of the other. This means there must exist a scalar \( c \) such that:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ k \end{pmatrix} = c \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This gives us the system of equations:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3 = c \cdot 1 \quad \text{and} \quad k = c \cdot 2
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m From the first equation, we can solve for \( c \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m c = 3
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
