[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the remainder when \(24^{50} - 15^{50}\) is divided by 13, we can use properties of modular arithmetic. Specifically, we will use the fact that \(24 \equiv -1 \pmod{13}\) and \(15 \equiv 6 \pmod{13}\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, let's rewrite the expression \(24^{50} - 15^{50}\) using these congruences:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[24^{50} - 15^{50} \equiv (-1)^{50} - 6^{50} \pmod{13}.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since \((-1)^{50} = 1\), we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[24^{50} - 15^{50} \equiv 1 - 6^{50} \pmod{13}.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we need to find the values of \(6^{50} \pmod{13}\). We can do this by computing the powers of 6 modulo 13:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[6^1 \equiv 6 \pmod{13},\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[6^2 \equiv 36 \equiv 10 \pmod{13},\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[6^3 \equiv 6 \cdot 10 = 60 \equiv 5 \pmod{13},\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[6^4 \equiv 6 \cdot 5 = 30 \equiv 4 \pmod{13},\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[6^5 \equiv 6 \cdot 4 = 24 \equiv 12 \equiv -1 \pmod{13},\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[6^6 \equiv 6 \cdot (-1) = -6 \equiv 7 \pmod{13},\]
