[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the number of vertical asymptotes of the function \( y = \frac{1}{x^3-3x^2+3x-1} \), we need to identify the points where the function approaches infinity as \( x \) approaches a certain value. Vertical asymptotes occur where the denominator of a rational function is zero, provided that the numerator is not zero at those points.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, let's factor the denominator \( x^3 - 3x^2 + 3x - 1 \). We can use the Rational Root Theorem to test possible rational roots. The possible rational roots are the factors of the constant term (-1) divided by the factors of the leading coefficient (1), which are \( \pm 1 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Testing \( x = 1 \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 1^3 - 3(1)^2 + 3(1) - 1 = 1 - 3 + 3 - 1 = 0 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, \( x = 1 \) is a root.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we factor \( x - 1 \) out of the polynomial \( x^3 - 3x^2 + 3x - 1 \). We use synthetic division to divide \( x^3 - 3x^2 + 3x - 1 \) by \( x - 1 \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \begin{array}{r|rrrr}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1 & 1 & -3 & 3 & -1 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m   &   & 1 & -2 & 1 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \hline
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m   & 1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \end{array}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The quotient is \( x^2 - 2x + 1 \), so we can write:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ x^3 - 3x^2 + 3x - 1 = (x - 1)(x^2 - 2x + 1) \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The polynomial \( x^2 - 2x + 1 \) can be factored further as:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ x^2 - 2x + 1 = (x - 1)^2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the denominator factors as:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ x^3 - 3x^2 + 3x - 1 = (x - 1)^3 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, the function becomes:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ y = \frac{1}{(x - 1)^3} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The vertical asymptotes occur where the denominator is zero, besides the point \( x = 1 \). However, since \( (x - 1)^3 \) is zero at \( x = 1 \), the function does not have a vertical asymptote at \( x = 1 \). The only point where the function approaches infinity or negative infinity is at \( x = 1 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the number of vertical asymptotes is \(\boxed{1}\).<|endoftext|>
