[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - Since \(T\) is on side \(QR\) and \(QT = 6\), \(TR = 10\), and \(R\) is 16 units to the right of \(Q\), we can find the coordinates of \(T\) by solving for \(x\) in the directrix of the isosceles trapezoid. When \(P\) and \(Q\) are directly below \(T\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      x + 6 = 16 \implies x = 10
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - Now, we calculate the area ratio.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3. **Calculate the areas of the triangles:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The area of \(\triangle PQR\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      \text{Area of } \triangle PQR = \frac{1}{2} \times \text{base} \times \text{height} = \frac{1}{2} \times (16 - 0) \times 0 = 0
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The area of \(\triangle PQT\) is:
