[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find \(\log_{7}{2400}\) rounded to the nearest integer, we need to use the change of base formula for logarithms, which states:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \log_{a}{b} = \frac{\log_{c}{b}}{\log_{c}{a}}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We want to simplify \( \log_{7}{2400} \) using a common base. A convenient base is 10, so we use:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \log_{7}{2400} = \frac{\log_{10}{2400}}{\log_{10}{7}}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, we need to calculate \(\log_{10}{2400}\) and \(\log_{10}{7}\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \log_{10}{2400} \approx 3.3805
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \log_{10}{7} \approx 0.8451
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, we divide these two values:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \log_{7}{2400} = \frac{3.3805}{0.8451} \approx 3.9825
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we need to determine whether this value is close to the nearest integer. The integer closest to 3.9825 is 4.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the value of \(\log_{7}{2400}\) rounded to the nearest integer is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{4}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
