[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To rationalize the denominator of $\frac{3}{2\sqrt[3]{5}}$, we need to get rid of the cube root in the denominator. We can do this by multiplying both the numerator and the denominator by $\sqrt[3]{25}$, which is the third root of $5$:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \frac{3}{2\sqrt[3]{5}} \cdot \frac{\sqrt[3]{25}}{\sqrt[3]{25}} = \frac{3\sqrt[3]{25}}{2 \cdot 5^{1/3}} = \frac{3\sqrt[3]{25}}{2 \cdot 5^{1/3}} = \frac{3 \cdot 5^{1/3}}{2 \cdot 25^{1/3}} = \frac{3 \cdot 5^{1/3}}{2 \cdot 25^{1/3}}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we can combine the cube roots:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \frac{3 \cdot 5^{1/3}}{2 \cdot 25^{1/3}} = \frac{3 \cdot 5^{1/3}}{2 \cdot 5^{2/3}} = \frac{3}{2} \cdot \frac{5^{1/3}}{5^{2/3}} = \frac{3}{2} \cdot \frac{5^{1/3} \cdot 5^{1/2}}{5^{1/3} \cdot 5^{1/3}} = \frac{3}{2} \cdot \frac{5^{1/3 + 1/2}}{5^{1/3 (1 + 1/3)}} = \frac{3}{2} \cdot \frac{5^{5/6}}{5^{5/6}} = \frac{3}{2}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the rationalized form of the expression is $\frac{3}{2}$, where $A = 3$, $B = 5$, and $C = 2$. The final answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m A + B + C = 3 + 5 + 2 = 10
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
