[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m R_1(t) = \langle 4\cos t, \cos^2 t, 5\sin t, 6\cos^2 t \rangle
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We need to find the partial derivatives of \(\vec{R}_1\) with respect to \(t\) to get the vector field \(\vec{R}_1\) relative to the curve \(C\). Specifically, we need the partial derivatives of each component of \(\vec{R}_1\) with respect to \(t\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \frac{\partial R_1}{\partial t} = \frac{\partial}{\partial t} \langle 4\cos t, \cos^2 t, 5\sin t, 6\cos^2 t \rangle = \langle -4\sin t, -2\cos t \sin t, 5\cos t, 12\cos^3 t \rangle
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m These components form the vector field \(\vec{R}^1(t_0)\), where \(t_0\) is the image of \(t\) under the curve \(C\). A vector field \(\vec{R}^1(t_0)\) is parallel to \(\vec{R}_1\) if:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \vec{R}^1(t_0) = \alpha \vec{R}_1(t_0) \quad \text{for some constant } \alpha
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This implies:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \langle -4\sin t_0, -2\cos t_0 \sin t_0, 5\cos t_0, 12\cos^3 t_0 \rangle = \alpha \langle 4\cos t, \cos^2 t, 5\sin t, 6\cos^2 t \rangle
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Equating the corresponding components, we get the following system of equations:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m -4\sin t_0 = 4\cos t \quad \text{(1)}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m -2\cos t_0 \sin t_0 = \cos^2 t \quad \text{(2)}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
