[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 12\cos^3 t_0 = 6\cos^2 t \quad \text{(4)}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m From equation (1), we have \(\sin t_0 = -\cos t\). From equation (2), we know \(\cos^2 t_0 = \frac{1}{2}\). From equation (3), we have \(\cos t_0 = \frac{1}{2}\tan t_0\), so \(\cos t_0 = \sin^2 t_0 = \frac{t^2}{1+t^2}\). From equation (4), we have \(\cos^3 t_0 = \cos t_0 \cdot \cos^2 t = \frac{t}{1+t^2}\), so \(\cos^3 t_0 = \frac{t}{1+t^2}\) and \(\cos t_0 = \frac{t}{1+t^2}\sec t\), so \(\cos t_0 = \frac{t}{1+t^2}\sec t = \frac{t}{1+t^2}\frac{1}{\cos t} = \frac{t}{\cos^2 t}\). Substituting \(\cos t_0 = \frac{t}{\cos^2 t}\) into equation (3), we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 5\cdot \frac{t}{\cos^2 t} = 5\sin t \implies t = \cos^2 t \implies t = \cos t
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This implies \(t = \tan t\), which is not possible for \(0 < t < \frac{\pi}{2}\). Instead, we can use the curve \(C_2\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
