[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine how many real numbers are not in the domain of the function \( f(x) = \frac{1}{x-64} + \frac{1}{x^2-64} + \frac{1}{x^3-64} \), we need to analyze the condition under which the function is undefined. The function is undefined when the denominators are zero.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, let's find the values of \( x \) that make the denominators zero:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. \( \frac{1}{x-64} \) is undefined when \( x = 64 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. \( \frac{1}{x^2-64} \) is undefined when \( x^2 - 64 = 0 \), which means \( x^2 = 64 \). So, \( x = 8 \) or \( x = -8 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3. \( \frac{1}{x^3-64} \) is undefined when \( x^3 - 64 = 0 \), which means \( x^3 = 64 \). So, \( x = 4 \) or \( x = -4 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
