[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Certainly! Let’s start by understanding the problem and breaking it down into manageable parts.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The solid described in the map can be understood as a combination of two parts involving a cylinder and a frustum (a part of a cone that is truncated).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ### Step 1: Identify the Base and Height of the Cylinder
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - The base of the cylinder has a radius (\(r\)) of 6 cm.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - The height (\(h\)) of the cylinder is given as 10 cm.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ### Step 2: Calculate the Surface Area of the Cylinder
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The surface area (SA) of a cylinder consists of two areas of the bases (which are circles) and the lateral surface area (LSA):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \text{Surface Area} = 2 \pi r^2 + 2 \pi r h \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Substituting the given values:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \text{Surface Area} = 2 \pi (6^2) + 2 \pi (6)(10) \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \text{Surface Area} = 2 \pi (36) + 2 \pi (60) \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \text{Surface Area} = 72 \pi + 120 \pi \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \text{Surface Area} = 192 \pi \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ### Step 3: Determine the Frustum Geometry
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The frustum is formed from the top of the cylinder and the part below the base. We need to calculate the lateral surface area of this part.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ### Step 4: Calculate the lateral Surface Area of the Frustum
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - Radius of the top base (\(R\)) of the frustum.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - Radius of the bottom base (\(r'\)) of the frustum.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - Slant height (\(l\)) of the frustum.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Given the \(6 \text{ cm}\) radius of the base of the cylinder, we can determine the radii of the top and bottom bases using similar triangles:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \frac{R}{6} = \frac{10}{10} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ R = 10 \text{ cm} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, for the bottom base:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \frac{10}{h} = \frac{10}{l} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ l = 2 \text{ cm} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Using the Pythagorean theorem to find \(r'\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ R^2 + r'^2 = l^2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ (10 \text{ cm})^2 + r'^2 = 2 \text{ cm}^2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 100 \text{ cm}^2 + r'^2 = 2 \text{ cm}^2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ r'^2 = 2 \text{ cm}^2 - 100 \text{ cm}^2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ r'^2 = 100 \text{ cm}^2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ r' = \sqrt{100 \text{ cm}^2} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ r' = 10 \text{ cm} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, calculate the lateral surface area of the frustum:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \text{Surface Area of Frustum} = 2 \pi R r + 2 \pi R l + 2 \pi \left(\sqrt{100 - r'^2}\right) r \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \text{Surface Area of Frustum} = 2 \pi (10)(10) + 2 \pi (10)(2) + 2 \pi (\sqrt{100 - 100}) \cdot 10 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \text{Surface Area of Frustum} = 200 \pi + 40 \pi + 0 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \text{Surface Area of Frustum} = 240 \pi \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ### Step 5: Calculate the Total Surface Area
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Combine the surface areas of the cylinder and the frustum:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \text{Total Surface Area} = \text{Surface Area of Cylinder} + \text{Surface Area of Frustum} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \text{Total Surface Area} = 192 \pi + 240 \pi \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \text{Total Surface Area} = 432 \pi \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the total surface area of the solid is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{432 \pi}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
