[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To evaluate \((\sqrt[3]{13})^6\), we need to understand the properties of exponents and root expressions. The expression \((\sqrt[3]{13})^6\) can be rewritten using the property of exponents that states \((\sqrt[n]{a})^m = a^m\sqrt[n]{a}\). Applying this property, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m (\sqrt[3]{13})^6 = 13^{6/3}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we simplify the exponent \(6/3\). Since the exponent is \(6/3\), which can be written as \(2\), we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 13^{6/3} = 13^2
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, we just need to calculate \(13^2\). We find that:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 13^2 = 169
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the final answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{169}
