[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Stephan has been busy at work writing all the possible rearrangements of the letters in his name. He is one bored boy. If he can write twelve rearrangements of his name every minute, how many hours does it take to write all the possible rearrangements of his name?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine how many hours it takes Stephan to write all the possible rearrangements of his name, we need to follow these steps:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. **Calculate the total number of rearrangements of the name "Stephan":**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    The name "Stephan" has 4 letters. The number of unique permutations of these 4 letters is given by the factorial of 4, denoted as \(4!\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    4! = 4 \times 3 \times 2 \times 1 = 24
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. **Determine how many minutes it takes Stephan to write all the rearrangements:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    Stephan writes 12 rearrangements each minute. Therefore, the total time in minutes is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    12 \text{ rearrangements} \times 1 \text{ minute per rearrangement} = 12 \text{ minutes}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3. **Convert the total time from minutes to hours:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
