[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the time in Denver when the train arrives, we need to break down the problem into smaller parts and solve it step by step.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. **Determine the time in Denver when the train leaves:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The train leaves New York at 2 p.m. (New York time).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - Denver is 45 hours away from New York (since we need to account for the difference in time zones).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - Therefore, the train leaves Denver at:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      2 \text{ p.m.} + 45 \text{ hours} = 2:45 \text{ p.m.}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. **Determine the time when the train arrives:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The train arrives in Denver 45 hours after it leaves.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - If the train leaves at 2:45 p.m., then arriving at Denver at 2:45 p.m. + 45 hours 45 minutes (since the train leaves in AM).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3. **Calculate the arrival time:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - Adding 45 hours to 2:45 p.m.:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      2:45 \text{ p.m.} + 45 \text{ hours} = 2:45 \text{ p.m.} + 45 \text{ hours} = 1:45 \text{ p.m.}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the time in Denver when the train arrives is \(\boxed{1:45 \text{ p.m.}}\).<|endoftext|>
