[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We can solve the problem by considering the complementary event. The product of the numbers is even only if at least one of the numbers is even. There are four even numbers (2, 4, 6, 8) and four odd numbers (1, 3, 5, 7) on each spinner.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m There is a 1/2 probability that the first spinner lands on an odd number and a 1/2 probability that the second spinner lands on an even number. Since the two events are independent, the probability that both spinners land on odd numbers is (1/2) * (1/2) = 1/4. Similarly, the probability that both spinners land on even numbers is also (1/2) * (1/2) = 1/4.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, the probability that at least one spinner lands on an even number is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ P(\text{at least one even}) = 1 - P(\text{both odd}) - P(\text{both even}) = 1 - \left(\frac{1}{2} \times \frac{1}{2}\right) - \left(\frac{1}{2} \times \frac{1}{2}\right) = 1 - \frac{1}{4} - \frac{1}{4} = \frac{3}{4} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the probability that the product of the two spinners' numbers is even is \(\boxed{\frac{3}{4}}\).<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \frac{2}{3}
