[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the ordered pair \((p, q)\) such that \(\mathbf{M}^2 = p \mathbf{M} + q \mathbf{I}\), we start by calculating \(\mathbf{M}^2\). Given the matrix \(\mathbf{M} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 7 \\ -3 & -1 \end{pmatrix}\), we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \mathbf{M}^2 = \mathbf{M} \cdot \mathbf{M} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 7 \\ -3 & -1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 7 \\ -3 & -1 \end{pmatrix}.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The product of two matrices is computed as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 7 \\ -3 & -1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 7 \\ -3 & -1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} (2 \cdot 2 + 7 \cdot -3) & (2 \cdot 7 + 7 \cdot -1) \\ (-3 \cdot 2 + -1 \cdot -3) & (-3 \cdot 7 + -1 \cdot -1) \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 - 21 & 14 - 7 \\ -6 + 3 & -21 + 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -19 & 7 \\ -3 & -20 \end{pmatrix}.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \mathbf{M}^2 = \begin{pmatrix} -19 & 7 \\ -3 & -20 \end{pmatrix}.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we need to find scalars \(p\) and \(q\) such that:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \mathbf{M}^2 = p \mathbf{M} + q \mathbf{I}.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since \(\mathbf{I} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}\), we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m p \mathbf{M} + q \mathbf{I} = p \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 7 \\ -3 & -1 \end{pmatrix} + q \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 2p + q & 7p \\ -3p & -q \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} q & 0 \\ 0 & q \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 2p + q + q & 7p + 0 \\ -3p + 0 & -q + q \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 2p + 2q & 7p \\ -3p & 0 \end{pmatrix}.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Equating this to \(\mathbf{M}^2\), we get the following system of equations:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2p + 2q = -19,
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 7p = 7,
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m -3p = -3.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m From the second equation, we find:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 7p = 7 \implies p = 1.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m From the third equation, we find:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m -3p = -3 \implies p = 1.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We substitute \(p = 1\) into the first equation:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2(1) + 2q = -19 \implies 2 + 2q = -19 \implies 2q = -21 \implies q = -\frac{21}{2}.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the ordered pair \((p, q)\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{\left(1, -\frac{21}{2}\right)}.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
