[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the shape described by the equation \(\theta = c\) in spherical coordinates \((\rho, \theta, \phi)\), we need to understand the relationship between Cartesian coordinates \((x, y, z)\) and spherical coordinates \((\rho, \theta, \phi)\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The relationship is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m x = \rho \sin \phi \cos \theta, \quad y = \rho \sin \phi \sin \theta, \quad z = \rho \cos \phi
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since \(\theta = c\), we substitute \(\theta = c\) into the equations for \(\phi\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \phi = \begin{cases} 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \phi_0 & \text{if } c \leq \frac{\pi}{2} \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \pi - c - \phi_0 & \text{if } c > \frac{\pi}{2}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \end{cases}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m In the case where \(c \leq \frac{\pi}{2}\), we have \(\phi = \phi_0\). So, the equation \(\theta = c\) simplifies to \(\phi = \phi_0\), indicating a circular arc around the \(z\)-axis.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m In the case where \(c > \frac{\pi}{2}\), \(\phi\) ranges from \(\pi - c\) to \(\pi\). This means there is a circular arc on each side of the \(z\)-axis, resulting in a circle on the \(xy\)-plane with a radius equal to the smaller half of the circumference on the side of the \(z\)-axis.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m However, since the problem asks for the equation \(\theta = c\) in spherical coordinates, we need to consider the case where \(c \leq \frac{\pi}{2}\) which gives us the equation \(\theta = \phi_0\). This indicates a circular arc around the \(z\)-axis.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the shape described by the equation \(\theta = c\) in spherical coordinates is a circle. The correct answer is (C) Plane.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, the final answer is \boxed{C}.<|endoftext|>
