[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the matrix \(\mathbf{P}\) such that \(\mathbf{P} \mathbf{v}\) is the projection of \(\mathbf{v}\) onto the \(y\)-axis for any vector \(\mathbf{v}\), we need to understand what the projection onto the \(y\)-axis involves. The projection of a vector \(\mathbf{v}\) onto the \(y\)-axis is given by the fraction of the vector \(\mathbf{v}\) that falls between itself and the origin in the \(\mathbf{v}\)-\(\mathbf{v}\) plane.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The projection of \(\mathbf{v}\) onto the \(y\)-axis is \(\mathbf{v} \cdot (\mathbf{v} - \mathbf{0}) / |\mathbf{v}|\), where \(\mathbf{0}\) is the origin. However, the standard form for the projection onto the \(y\)-axis is simply \(\mathbf{v}\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the matrix \(\mathbf{P}\) such that \(\mathbf{P} \mathbf{v}\) is \(\mathbf{v}\), we can set \(\mathbf{v} = e_i\) (the ith standard basis vector), where \(i\) ranges from 1 to 3, and solve for \(\mathbf{P}\) as:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \mathbf{P} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This is because for each element of the standard basis in the mixed order (e.g., \(\mathbf{v} \cdot (\mathbf{v} - \mathbf{0}) / |\mathbf{v}|\)), the corresponding element in the matrix must be 1 to correctly project onto the \(y\)-axis in the mixed order.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the matrix \(\mathbf{P}\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix}}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m [Ground Truth]
