[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Certainly! Let's compute $\cos 45^\circ$.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The cosine of an angle in a right triangle can be calculated using the concept of sine as the ratio of the adjacent side to the hypotenuse. In a 45-45-90 triangle, both angles are $45^\circ$, and the two legs (adjacent and opposite) are equal in length. Therefore, the cosine of an $45^\circ$ angle is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \cos 45^\circ = \frac{\text{adjacent}}{\text{hypotenuse}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the value of $\cos 45^\circ$ is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
