[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine what time it will be after 6666 seconds, we need to convert 6666 seconds into a format that we can work with.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, we note that 6666 seconds is equivalent to \( \frac{6666}{3 \times 60 \times 60} \) minutes because there are 3600 seconds in an hour and 60 seconds in a minute. Simplifying this:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \frac{6666}{3 \times 60 \times 60} = \frac{6666}{10800} = \frac{6666 \div 6}{10800 \div 6} = \frac{1111}{1800}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, 6666 seconds is equal to approximately \(\frac{1111}{1800}\) minutes. Next, we convert this into hours, minutes, and seconds. We do this by dividing 1111 by 60 and finding the remainder:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1111 \div 60 = 18 \text{ remainder } 31
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This means that the conversion is exactly 18 hours and 31 minutes, or \(\frac{94}{6}\) hours, since there are 24 hours in a day and we can ignore the remainder part for this problem. So, we have 18 hours and 31 minutes.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, we need to convert 31 minutes into hours and minutes. Since 31 minutes is equivalent to \(\frac{31}{60}\) hours:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 31 \div 60 = 0 \text{ remainder } 31
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, 31 minutes is exactly 2 hours and 31 minutes.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the total time after 6666 seconds is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \frac{18 \text{ hours} + 2 \text{ hours} + 31 \text{ minutes}}{24 \text{ hours/day} \times 60 \text{ minutes/hour}} = \frac{59 \text{ hours} + 31 \text{ minutes}}{1440 \text{ minutes/day}} = \frac{59}{1440} \text{ days} + \frac{31 \text{ minutes}}{1440 \text{ minutes}} = \frac{31}{512 \text{ days}} + \frac{31 \text{ minutes}}{1440 \text{ minutes}} = \frac{31 \times 6}{1440} \text{ hours} + \frac{31 \text{ minutes}}{1440 \text{ minutes}} = \frac{186 \text{ hours}}{1440} \text{ hours} + \frac{31 \times 6}{1440 \text{ minutes}} = 1 \text{ hour} + \frac{31 \times 6}{1440 \text{ minutes}}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Converting 1 hour (60 minutes) to a multiple of 60 minutes, we get 60 + 2 = 62 minutes. Therefore, 6666 seconds is equivalent to 1 hour and \(62\) minutes.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, the time it will be in 6666 seconds is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{1:062 \text{ PM}}
