[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine how many minutes it would take Jordan to run 5 miles, we need to follow these steps:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. **Find Jordan's running speed:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    Jordan ran 2 miles in 24 minutes. Therefore, Jordan's running speed is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \text{Speed} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Time}} = \frac{2 \text{ miles}}{24 \text{ minutes}} = \frac{1 \text{ mile}}{12 \text{ minutes}}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. **Calculate the time it takes Jordan to run 1 mile:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    Using Jordan's speed, we can find out how long it takes him to run one mile:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \text{Time for 1 mile} = \frac{1 \text{ mile}}{\frac{1 \text{ mile}}{12 \text{ minutes}}} = 12 \text{ minutes}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3. **Determine the time for Jordan to run 5 miles:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    To find out how long it takes Jordan to run 5 miles, we multiply his running speed by 5:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \text{Time for 5 miles} = 5 \text{ miles} \times 12 \text{ minutes per mile} = 60 \text{ minutes}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, it would take Jordan \boxed{60} minutes to run 5 miles.<|endoftext|>
