[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To solve for \( f^{-1}(f^{-1}(3)) \), we need to understand the properties of inverse functions. Specifically, we need to know that for an inverse function \( f^{-1} \), applying the inverse function twice returns the original input value. Mathematically, this can be written as \( f^{-1} \circ f = f \circ f^{-1} = \text{identity function} \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Given that \( f(1) = 2 \), \( f(4) = 3 \), and \( f(7) = 4 \), we can use the property of inverse functions to find \( f^{-1}(3) \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, let's find \( f(3) \) using the information \( f(4) = 3 \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ f(4) = f^{-1}(f(1)) \implies 3 = f^{-1}(2) \implies f^{-1}(3) = 2. \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, \( f^{-1}(3) = 2 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we need to find \( f^{-1}(2) \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ f^{-1}(f(1)) = f^{-1}(2) \implies f^{-1}(2) = 1. \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, \( f^{-1}(f^{-1}(3)) = f^{-1}(2) = 1 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The final answer is \(\boxed{1}\).<|endoftext|>
