[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine how many positive integers \( n \) satisfy the inequality \( 200 < n^2 < 900 \), we need to find the range of \( n \) values that satisfy this inequality.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, let's solve the inequality \( 200 < n^2 < 900 \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. Find the approximate values of the square roots of the bounds:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \sqrt{200} \approx 14.14 \quad \text{and} \quad \sqrt{900} \approx 30.00
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. Set up the inequalities for \( n \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    14.14 < n < 30.00
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3. Interpolate within this interval to find the value of \( n \) that also gives \( 200 < n^2 \). We use the midpoint of the interval:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    n \approx 17.07
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 4. Since \( n \) must be an integer, we take the nearest integers:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    n = 17 \quad \text{or} \quad n = 18
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 5. Calculate \( n^2 \) for these values:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    17^2 = 289
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    18^2 = 324
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 6. Verify these values:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    289 < 200 < 900 \quad \text{(True)}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    324 < 900 < 900 \quad \text{(True)}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since \( n = 18 \) also satisfies the inequality \( 200 < n^2 < 900 \), the positive integer \( n \) that satisfies the inequality is \( 18 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the number of positive integers \( n \) that satisfy \( 200 < n^2 < 900 \) is \(\boxed{1}\).<|endoftext|>
