[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find a nonzero \( p \) such that the quadratic equation \( px^2 - 12x + 4 = 0 \) has only one solution, we need to determine when a quadratic equation has a double root. A quadratic equation \( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \) has a double root if and only if its discriminant is zero. The discriminant \(\Delta\) of the quadratic equation \( px^2 + bx + c = 0 \) is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \Delta = b^2 - 4ac
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m For the equation \( px^2 - 12x + 4 = 0 \), the coefficients are \( a = p \), \( b = -12 \), and \( c = 4 \). The discriminant is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \Delta = (-12)^2 - 4(p)(4) = 144 - 16p
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m For the quadratic equation to have only one solution, the discriminant must be zero:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 144 - 16p = 0
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Solving for \( p \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 144 = 16p
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m p = \frac{144}{16} = 9
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the value of \( p \) that ensures the equation \( px^2 - 12x + 4 = 0 \) has only one solution is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{9}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
