[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the area of \(\triangle ABC\) with the given conditions, we start by noting that \(AB = AC\) and the altitude \(AD\) bisects the base \(BC\), so \(BD = DC = 5\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, we find the length of \(BC\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m BC = BD + DC = 5 + 5 = 10
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we know that in any triangle, the area can be calculated using the formula:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} \times \text{base} \times \text{height}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Here, the base is \(BC\) and the height is \(AD\). Substituting the known values, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} \times 10 \times AD
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since \(AB = AC\), the perpendicular height from \(A\) to \(BC\) will be the same as the height from \(B\) to \(AC\) because \(D\) is the midpoint. This means \(AD\) is the same as the height from \(A\) to \(BC\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The area of \(\triangle ABC\) is half the area of \(\triangle BDC\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Area of } \triangle BDC = \frac{1}{2} \times BC \times AD = \frac{1}{2} \times 10 \times AD
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m However, we do not need to find the exact height of \(\triangle BDC\) to determine the total area:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Total Area} = \text{Area of } \triangle ABC = \frac{1}{2} \times BC \times AD
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m But we can directly use the fact that \(AD\) is the same as \(BC \times \text{height from } A \text{ to } BC\), which simplifies to \(AD = BC \times \frac{1}{2} = 10 \times \frac{1}{2} = 5\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the area of \(\triangle ABC\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{100}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
