[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine how many apples Jimmy needs to weigh the same as 28 oranges, we need to follow these steps:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. **Understand the given information:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - \(7\) oranges weigh the same as \(5\) apples.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. **Find the weight of one orange:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - Since 7 oranges weigh the same as \(5\) apples, we can set up a proportion:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      \frac{7 \text{ oranges}}{5 \text{ apples}} = \frac{28 \text{ oranges}}{x \text{ apples}}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - Solving for \(x\) (the number of apples), we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      x = \frac{28 \text{ oranges} \times 5 \text{ apples}}{7 \text{ oranges}} = \frac{140}{7} = 20
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, Jimmy needs \(\boxed{20}\) apples to weigh the same as 28 oranges.<|endoftext|>
