[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 9118, 12173, and 33182, we can use the Euclidean algorithm. This algorithm is efficient and involves a series of division steps where we replace the larger number by its remainder when divided by the smaller number, until one of the numbers becomes zero. The other number at that step will be the GCD.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Here are the steps:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. Divide 12173 by 9118 and find the remainder.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    12173 = 9118 \cdot 1 + 3055 \implies \text{Remainder} = 3055
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    So, we replace 12173 with 3055 and 9118 with 9118.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. Divide 9118 by 3055 and find the remainder.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    9118 = 3055 \cdot 3 + 4066 \implies \text{Remainder} = 4066
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    So, we replace 3055 with 3055 and 9118 with 4066.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3. Divide 3055 by 4066 and find the remainder.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    3055 = 4066 \cdot 0 + 3055 \implies \text{Remainder} = 0
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    So, we replace 3055 with 0 and 4066 with 4066.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since the remainder is now 0, the GCD is the last non-zero remainder, which is 3055.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the greatest common divisor of 9118, 12173, and 33182 is \boxed{3055}.<|endoftext|>
