[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Find the intersection of the lines $9x-4y=6$ and $7x+y=17$. Express your answer as an ordered pair $(x,y)$.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the intersection of the lines \(9x - 4y = 6\) and \(7x + y = 17\), we need to solve the system of linear equations simultaneously. Let's denote the equations as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 9x - 4y = 6 \quad \text{(1)}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 7x + y = 17 \quad \text{(2)}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, we will eliminate one of the variables by using the method of elimination. We can eliminate \(y\) by making the coefficients of \(y\) in both equations the same. To do this, we multiply equation (1) by 1 and equation (2) by 4:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 9x - 4y = 6 \quad \text{(1)}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 28x + 4y = 68 \quad \text{(3)}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, we add equations (1) and (3):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m (9x - 4y) + (28x + 4y) = 6 + 68
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 37x = 74
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m x = 2
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now that we have \(x = 2\), we substitute this value back into equation (2) to find \(y\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 7(2) + y = 17
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 14 + y = 17
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m y = 3
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, the intersection point of the lines is \((2, 3)\). Therefore, the final answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{(2, 3)}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
