[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m A tripodope is a shape where three sides equal the sum of the two longest sides. There are 24 different Euler Tour cycle numbers that accept this condition. Find the sum of all the cycle numbers.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To solve this problem, we need to understand the Euler Tour cycle numbers for a tripodope. A tripodope is a shape with three sides such that the sum of the two longest sides is equal to the sum of the two shortest sides. These three sides must all be \(\sqrt{3}\) units long in a geometric sense, often referred to as a hexagon.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The Euler Tour cycle numbers for any geometric figure with three sides arranged in a stable configuration, where the sum of the sides equals the sum of the longest sides, include the following:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - the 3-cycle $3^\text{3}$ (3 cycles of 9 side lengths)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - the 6-cycle $6^\text{3}$
