[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m A certain organism begins as two cells. Each cell splits and becomes two cells at the end of three days. At the end of another three days, every cell of the organism splits and becomes two cells. This process lasts for a total of 15 days, and no cells die during this time. How many cells are there at the end of the $15^\text{th}$ day?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the number of cells at the end of the 15th day, we need to understand the pattern of cell division. According to the problem, each cell splits into two cells after three days, and this process repeats every three days. Therefore, the number of cells at the end of each 3-day cycle can be calculated as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. After 3 days, each cell becomes 2 cells.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. After 6 days, each cell becomes \(2 \times 2 = 4\) cells.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3. After 9 days, each cell becomes \(2 \times 4 = 8\) cells.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 4. After 12 days, each cell becomes \(2 \times 8 = 16\) cells.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 5. After 15 days, each cell becomes \(2 \times 16 = 32\) cells.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, the number of cells at the end of the 15th day is \(32\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the answer is \(\boxed{32}\).<|endoftext|>
