[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the units digit of \(16^{17} \times 17^{18} \times 18^{19}\), we can simplify the problem by observing the pattern in the units digits of the powers of certain numbers. The units digit of a number is the remainder when the number is divided by 10.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, let's find the units digits of the powers of 16, 17, and 18:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \begin{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 16^1 & = 16 \quad \text{(units digit is 6)}, \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 16^2 & = 256 \quad \text{(units digit is 6)}, \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 16^3 & = 4096 \quad \text{(units digit is 6)}, \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 16^4 & = 65536 \quad \text{(units digit is 6)}, \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 16^5 & = 1048576 \quad \text{(units digit is 6)}, \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{and so on.}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \end{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m From this, we see that the units digit of \(16^n\) is always 6 for any positive integer \(n\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{For any integer } n, \text{ the units digit of } 16^n \text{ is always 6.}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Similarly, we can find the units digits of \(17^{17}\) and \(18^{19}\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \begin{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 17^1 & = 17 \quad \text{(units digit is 7)}, \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 17^2 & = 289 \quad \text{(units digit is 9)}, \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 17^3 & = 4913 \quad \text{(units digit is 3)}, \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 17^4 & = 83521 \quad \text{(units digit is 1)}, \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 17^5 & = 1119857 \quad \text{(units digit is 7)}, \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{and so on.}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \end{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m From this, we see that the units digit of \(17^n\) is always 7 for any positive integer \(n\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Let's find the units digit of \(18^{19}\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \begin{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 18^1 & = 18 \quad \text{(units digit is 8)}, \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 18^2 & = 324 \quad \text{(units digit is 4)}, \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 18^3 & = 5832 \quad \text{(units digit is 2)}, \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 18^4 & = 116328 \quad \text{(units digit is 8)}, \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 18^5 & = 3073288 \quad \text{(units digit is 8)}, \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{and so on.}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \end{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We observe that the units digit of \(18^n\) changes every 4 terms: 8, 4, 2, 8, 8, ...
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since \(17^{17}\) and \(18^{19}\) are both obtained by doubling \(18^{19}\), we need to find the units digit of \(2 \times 18^{19}\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2 \times 8 = 16 \quad \text{(units digit is 6)}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2 \times 4 = 8 \quad \text{(units digit is 8)}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
