[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m A jet plane is attempting to fly due west for an average speed of 400 miles per hour. The plane has a constant headwind that increases its velocity by 25 mph every hour. What is the distance the plane will travel in 6 hours from an initial location? To determine the distance the jet plane will travel in 6 hours, we need to break the problem into two parts: the headwind effect and the speed gain due to the increasing velocity.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. **Calculate the velocity of the plane after 6 hours:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    The plane's velocity due to the increased wind is 25 mph per hour. Therefore, after 6 hours, the velocity due to the wind is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    25 \text{ mph/hour} \times 6 \text{ hours} = 150 \text{ mph}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. **Calculate the total distance traveled:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    The velocity due to the wind is 150 mph in the initial 6 hours. The next 6 hours, assuming the wind has not increased the velocity completely (since the problem does not specify wind speed or time tending to zero), the plane's velocity will be 400 mph. The total distance traveled will be the sum of the distances traveled in these two 6-hour intervals:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    6 \text{ hours} \times 400 \text{ miles/hour} + 6 \text{ hours} \times 150 \text{ mph} = 2400 \text{ miles} + 900 \text{ miles} = 3300 \text{ miles}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the distance the plane will travel in 6 hours from an initial location is \(\boxed{3300}\) miles.<|endoftext|>
