[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the least three-digit 9-heavy whole number, we need to follow these steps:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. Identify the smallest three-digit number.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. Determine the remainder of this number when divided by 9.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3. Check if this remainder is greater than 5.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 4. If it is, then the number itself is 9-heavy.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 5. If the remainder is not greater than 5, continue to the next smallest number and repeat the process.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The smallest three-digit number is 100. Now, we find the remainder when 100 is divided by 9:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 100 \div 9 = 11 \text{ remainder } 1 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, the remainder is 1. Since 1 is not greater than 5, the number itself is 9-heavy.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To confirm, we check the next numbered 9-heavy number, which is 198. We find the remainder when 198 is divided by 9:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 198 \div 9 = 22 \text{ remainder } 0 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, the remainder is 0, which is not greater than 5. Therefore, 198 is also 9-heavy.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the smallest three-digit 9-heavy whole number is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{198}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
