[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To convert the decimal number \(123_{10}\) to base 5, we need to repeatedly divide the number by 5 and keep track of the remainders. Here are the steps:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. Divide 123 by 5:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    123 \div 5 = 24 \text{ remainder } 3
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    So, the quotient is 24 and the remainder is 3.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. Divide 24 by 5:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    24 \div 5 = 4 \text{ remainder } 4
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    So, the quotient is 4 and the remainder is 4.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3. Divide 4 by 5:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    4 \div 5 = 0 \text{ remainder } 4
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    So, the quotient is 0 and the remainder is 4.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, we have the remainders from the divisions: 4, 4, and 3. Reading these remainders from bottom to top gives us the base 5 representation of the decimal number \(123_{10}\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
