[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the constant \( b \) in the function \( y = a \sin(bx + c) \), we need to understand the behavior of the sine function and relate it to the given graph.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The function \( y = a \sin(bx + c) \) is shifted vertically by a constant \( c \) from the sine function \( y = a \sin(bx) \). The angle \( \theta = b \) represents the phase shift of the sine function. A vertical shift by \( c \) from \( y = a \sin(bx) \) gives us the function \( y = a \sin(bx + c) \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m From the graph, we can see that the function \( y = 2 \sin(4x + \pi/2) \) has a period of \( \pi \) (since the sine function has a period of \( 2\pi \)) and a phase shift of \( \pi/8 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m In general, a sine function with a vertical shift of \( c \) can be written as \( y = a \sin(bx + c) \). Here, \( c \) can be any real number, but if we assume the simplest linear shift (which corresponds to \( c = 0 \)), the function becomes \( y = a \sin(bx) \). Since the problem does not specify a non-zero \( c \), we can consider the case where \( c = 0 \), but it should be understood in the context of the equation \( y = a \sin(bx + c) \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Given that the phase shift is a constant, and the overall graph shows a basic sine wave with a period of \( \pi \) and no vertical shift, the phase shift \( \theta = b \) from \( y = 2 \sin(4x + \pi/2) \) is 0 radians (or 0 cycles). This means \( b = 0 \).
