[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Our school's girls volleyball team has 14 players, including a set of 3 triplets: Missy, Lauren, and Liz.  In how many ways can we choose 6 starters if the only restriction is that not all 3 triplets can be in the starting lineup?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the number of ways to choose 6 starters from a volleyball team of 14 players, including a set of 3 triplets (Missy, Lauren, and Liz), we can break the problem down into two parts: choosing 3 players from the remaining 11 players (10 players except Missy, Lauren, and Liz) and then choosing 3 out of the remaining 7 players (from the 11 players minus the 3 triplets).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m **Step 1: Choose 3 players out of the remaining 11 players.**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The number of ways to choose 3 players from 11 is given by the combination formula \( \binom{n}{k} = \frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!} \), where \( n \) is the total number of items to choose from, \( k \) is the number of items to choose, and \( ! \) denotes factorial. So, the number of ways to choose 3 players from 11 is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \binom{11}{3} = \frac{11!}{3!(11-3)!} = \frac{11!}{3!8!} = \frac{11 \times 10 \times 9}{3 \times 2 \times 1} = 165
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m **Step 2: Choose 3 players out of the remaining 7 players.**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The number of ways to choose 3 players from 7 is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \binom{7}{3} = \frac{7!}{3!(7-3)!} = \frac{7!}{3!4!} = \frac{7 \times 6 \times 5}{3 \times 2 \times 1} = 35
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m **Step 3: Multiply the number of ways to choose the 3 players from the remaining 11 players by the number of ways to choose the 3 players from the remaining 7 players.**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The total number of ways to choose 6 starters is the product of the two combinations we calculated:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \binom{11}{3} \times \binom{7}{3} = 165 \times 35 = 5775
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the number of ways to choose 6 starters from the 14 players, including a set of 3 triplets, is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{5775}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
