[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the number of degrees in the measure of angle \( x \), we need to use the fact that the sum of the angles in a triangle is always 180 degrees. The angles in the triangle are:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - Angle \( x \)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - Angle \( 26^\circ \) (from the parallelogram)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - Angle \( 23^\circ \) (from the smaller triangle)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, we can write the equation for the sum of the angles as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ x + 26^\circ + 23^\circ = 180^\circ \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, we combine the known angle measures:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ x + 49^\circ = 180^\circ \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find \( x \), we subtract 49 degrees from both sides of the equation:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ x = 180^\circ - 49^\circ \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ x = 131^\circ \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the measure of angle \( x \) is \(\boxed{131}\).<|endoftext|>
