[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m A shirt regularly priced at $\$30$ is on sale for $20\%$ off. Mary has a coupon that will take another $25\%$ off of the sale price. What single percent discount would give the same final price as the two successive discounts?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the single percent discount that would give the same final price as the two successive discounts, we need to follow these steps:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. **Calculate the sale price of the shirt after the first discount of 20% off:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    The original price of the shirt is \$30. A 20% discount means the price after the first discount is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    30 \times (1 - 0.20) = 30 \times 0.80 = 24 \text{ dollars}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. **Calculate the final price after the second discount of 25% off the sale price:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    The sale price is \$24. A 25% discount means the price after the second discount is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    24 \times (1 - 0.25) = 24 \times 0.75 = 18 \text{ dollars}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3. **Determine the single percent discount equivalent to the final price after both discounts:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    Let \( x \) be the single percent discount that would give the same final price. After the first discount, the price will be:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    30 \times \left(1 - \frac{x}{100}\right)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    After the second discount, the price will be:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    30 \times \left(1 - \frac{x}{100}\right) \times \left(1 - \frac{25}{100}\right) = 30 \times \left(1 - \frac{x}{100}\right) \times 0.75 = 21 \times \left(1 - \frac{x}{100}\right)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    We want this final price to be equal to \$18, so we set up the equation:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    21 \times \left(1 - \frac{x}{100}\right) = 18
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    Solving for \( x \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    1 - \frac{x}{100} = \frac{18}{21} = \frac{6}{7}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \frac{x}{100} = 1 - \frac{6}{7} = \frac{1}{7}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    x = 100 \times \frac{1}{7} = \frac{100}{7} \approx 14.29
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the single percent discount that would give the same final price as the two successive discounts is \(\boxed{14.29}\).<|endoftext|>
