[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the number of positive integers \( x \) for which \( x^2 + 6x + 9 \) is between 20 and 40, we start by simplifying the expression. Notice that \( x^2 + 6x + 9 \) can be factored as a perfect square:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m x^2 + 6x + 9 = (x + 3)^2
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the inequality becomes:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m (x + 3)^2 \in [20, 40]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we find the range of \( x + 3 \) by taking the square root of 20 and 40:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 20 \leq (x + 3)^2 \leq 40
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Taking the square root of each part of the inequality, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \sqrt{20} \leq |x + 3| \leq \sqrt{40}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since \(\sqrt{20} = 2\sqrt{5}\) and \(\sqrt{40} = 2\sqrt{10}\), the ranges for \( |x + 3| \) are:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m -2\sqrt{5} \leq x + 3 \leq 2\sqrt{10}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Subtracting 3 from all parts of the inequality, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m -2\sqrt{5} - 3 \leq x \leq 2\sqrt{10} - 3
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since \( x \) must be a positive integer, we need to find the integer values of \( x \) that satisfy this range. We start by approximating the values:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m -2\sqrt{5} \approx -4.47 \quad \text{and} \quad 2\sqrt{10} \approx 6.32
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, the integer values for \( x \) are:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m -4, -3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since \( x \) must be positive, we exclude -2 and -1, leaving us with:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 0, 1, 2, 3
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the positive integers \( x \) that satisfy the inequality are:
