[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the remainder when \(99 \times 101\) is divided by 9, we can use properties of modular arithmetic. Specifically, we can use the fact that the product of two numbers \(a\) and \(b\) modulo 9 is the same as the product of their remainders modulo 9.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, we find the remainders of 99 and 101 when divided by 9:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[99 \div 9 = 11 \text{ remainder } 0 \implies 99 \equiv 0 \pmod{9}\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[101 \div 9 = 11 \text{ remainder } 2 \implies 101 \equiv 2 \pmod{9}\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, we calculate the product of these remainders:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[99 \times 101 \equiv 0 \times 2 = 0 \pmod{9}\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the remainder when \(99 \times 101\) is divided by 9 is \(\boxed{0}\).<|endoftext|>
