[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the smallest possible degree of \( q(x) \) for which the rational function \( \frac{2x^7 - 5x^4 + 3x^3 - x + 5}{q(x)} \) has a horizontal asymptote, we need to analyze the degrees of the polynomials in the numerator and the denominator.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The degree of a polynomial is the highest power of \( x \) in the polynomial. The horizontal asymptote of a rational function \( \frac{P(x)}{Q(x)} \) depends on the degrees of the polynomials \( P(x) \) and \( Q(x) \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. **Identify the degrees of the polynomials:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The numerator of the function \( 2x^7 - 5x^4 + 3x^3 - x + 5 \) has the highest power of \( x \), which is \( 7 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The denominator of the function \( Q(x) \) has the highest power of \( x \), which is \( 1 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. **Determine the horizontal asymptote:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The horizontal asymptote of a rational function is determined by the degrees of the numerator and the denominator. The horizontal asymptote will be \( y = \text{the larger degree of } Q(x) \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - In this case, the smaller degree is \( 1 \) (from the denominator), so the smallest possible degree of \( q(x) \) is \( 1 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the smallest possible degree of \( q(x) \) is \(\boxed{1}\).<|endoftext|>
