[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m r = 6\left\{ nj(\phi_n -\phi) \right\} + sj =  $$
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 4(\phi -(\phi_n -\phi)) = 8r. \label{eq1}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Some algebra yields two condition
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \begin{cases}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \phi_r-\phi_n = \frac{8}{n} (\phi-r) \\ 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \phi -\phi_n = \frac{4}{n} (\phi-r) 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \end{cases}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Then 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \begin{cases}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \frac{8}{n} (\phi_r -\phi_n )  &=  8r \\ 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m & \Leftrightarrow \phi_r-\phi_n  = \frac{8r}{4} \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m & \Leftrightarrow \phi_r - \phi_n =r \label{eq3}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \end{cases}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m so,
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m $ \phi  = \frac{8r n}{8r - n\phi}$
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m $ \boxed{\phi = \frac{8 r n}{8r - n \phi_1 - n\phi_2}.} $
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Putting this into a model equation one gets
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \begin{align}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m jl\circ_j\sum_{i \in im}(jLagr\sum_{j \in il}(y_il + jx_il)) &= 8nr\\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \Leftrightarrow \phi_j &= \frac{8 r n}{8r - n\frac{lr}{\omega(n)\omega(\omega(r))}-n\phi_1-n\phi_2} \cr
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \Leftrightarrow \label{eq4}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m  x_{il} &=\phi_il - \frac{8 r n\omega(\omega(n))}{8r - n\phi_1-n\phi_2}\\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \end{align}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The last step is simply because $\frac{x_{ij}}{y_j}$ is linear.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2) For multiple data... If there are $M$ multiple data one with $j = 1,...,M$, then obviously, sum of $x$ that is just multiple of $\sum_j x_{ij}$ 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So from Jensen's Inequality we get
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \begin{align}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \sum_{i \in im}(jLagr\sum_{j \in il}(y_il + jx_il)) = \sum_{i \in im}(jLagr\sum_{j = 1}^M(x_{il} + jx_il)) = jLagr\qquad\qquad\ge 0
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \end{align}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Which bring us to our point:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m $$\label{eq5}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \sum_{j = 1}^M x_{il} \ge jx_il \; \forall\, i , j
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m and hence, \begin{align}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m jl\circ_j\sum_{i \in im}(jLagr\sum_{j \in il}(y_il + jx_il)) &= \sum_il(jLagr\sum_{j \in im}(x_{ij} + jx_il)) \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m &= j\sum_il(x_{ij} + jx_il) \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m &= j[mL\sum_il(x_{ij}) + j\sum_ilx_il].
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \end{align}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The last equality is just thank x, so in fact the equality above definitely holds for $M = 1$ (thanks FF!).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Then the carga of node $A$ is necessarily $j$:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \begin{align}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \label{eq6}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \sum_{i \in im}(jLagr\sum_{j \in il}(y_il + jx_il)) = mj\sum_{i \in im}(x_{ij})= \sum_il(x_{ij}) .
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \end{align}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We notice only the sign of $jl$ works. and simply said at every node do the same thing.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Using Jordan Form, which indicates how to map a matrix $A=eA_2e^T$ to a diagonal matrix $L$, let's find the eigenvectors of $A$ and $A=JL$ 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \begin{align}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \alpha\beta  &+  \beta\gamma +\gamma\alpha = \beta+\gamma \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \alpha\gamma  &+ \gamma\beta  +\beta\alpha =  \beta +\alpha +\alpha \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \beta\alpha  &+ \alpha\gamma  +\gamma\beta = \alpha
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \end{align}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The exponentials of the eigenvectors must be satisfying the equation $\cosh(x)=1$ , from which we discover that 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \begin{align}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1-4x^2 &\le 0 \implies x^2 \ge \frac{1}{4} \\
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \end{align}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m By applying matrix transformations to $A=A^2-4A+4$, the resulting matrix is diagonal.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m One more nice solution is the cyclic $ A=( 1  2/3  4/3  5/6 7/6) 1 $( See if you are able to generalize these solutions).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
