[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To compute \(\tan 180^\circ\), we start by recalling the definition of the tangent function. The tangent of an angle in a right triangle is defined as the ratio of the length of the opposite side to the length of the adjacent side. That is, \(\tan \theta = \frac{\text{opposite}}{\text{adjacent}}\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m For \(\theta = 180^\circ\), the angle is in the position where the opposite side is on the negative y-axis and the adjacent side is on the positive x-axis. This is in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent value is negative.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, \(\tan 180^\circ = -1\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So the final answer is \(\boxed{-1}\).<|endoftext|>
