[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the units digit of \(17^{13} - 17\), we can start by examining the units digit of \(17^{13}\) and then understand how it interacts with subtracting 17.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, let's observe the units digits of the base numbers: 17, 18, 19, ..., 99. These numbers form a repeating cycle of 5. This is because \(17^7 = 193878717\), and \(193878717 \equiv 3 \pmod{10}\). Multiplying by more significant terms and reducing, we see the cycle is \(17, 18, 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24, 25, \ldots\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since \(17^{13} \equiv 7 \pmod{10}\) (as \(17^2 = 289 \equiv 9 \pmod{10}\) and \(17^3 = 4913 \equiv 3 \pmod{10}\), and \(17^4 = 83521 \equiv 1 \pmod{10}\), then \(17 \equiv 7 \pmod{10}\)), we have \(17^{13} \equiv 7 \pmod{10}\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, we need the units digit of \(17 - 17 = 0\). The units digit of a number and its subtraction does not affect the units digit of its final result. Therefore, the units digit of \(17^{13} - 17\) is \(\boxed{7}\).<|endoftext|>
