[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m What is $\dbinom{n}{1}$ for any positive integer $n$?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find \(\binom{n}{1}\) for any positive integer \(n\), we need to understand what \(\binom{n}{1}\) represents. The binomial coefficient \(\binom{n}{k}\) is defined as the number of ways to choose \(k\) elements from a set of \(n\) elements without regard to the order of selection. Here, \(k = 1\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The formula for \(\binom{n}{1}\) is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \binom{n}{1} = \frac{n!}{1!(n-1)!} = \frac{n!(n-1)!}{1! \cdot (n-1)!} = \frac{n \cdot (n-1)!}{1 \cdot (n-1)!} = n
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, for any positive integer \(n\), \(\binom{n}{1} = n\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The final answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{n}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m n
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
