[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m For a certain weekend, the weatherman predicts that it will rain with a $40\%$ probability on Saturday and a $50\%$ probability on Sunday. Assuming these probabilities are independent, what is the probability that it rains over the weekend (that is, on at least one of the days)? Express your answer as a percentage.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the probability that it rains over the weekend, we need to consider both days individually and then use the fact that the probabilities are independent.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, let's find the probability that it rains on exactly one day. The probability that it rains on both days is the product of the probabilities of rain on each day. Since the probabilities are given as \(40\%\) for Saturday and \(50\%\) for Sunday, the probability of rain on only one day is calculated as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m P(\text{rain on Saturday and not on Sunday}) \text{ and } P(\text{rain on Sunday and not on Saturday}) = P(\text{rain on Saturday}) \times P(\text{not Sunday}) + P(\text{not Friday}) \times P(\text{rain on Sunday})
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Each of these probabilities can be calculated as the product of the probabilities of each event:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m P(\text{rain on Saturday}) = 0.40, \quad P(\text{not Sunday}) = 1 - 0.50 = 0.50, \quad P(\text{not Friday}) = 1 - 0.60 = 0.40
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, we can find the probability of rain on only one day:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m P(\text{rain on Saturday and not on Sunday}) = 0.40 \times 0.50 + 0.40 \times 0.40 = 0.20 + 0.16 = 0.36
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The probability that it rains on at least one day is the complement of the probability that it does not rain on any of the days. Therefore, we calculate:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m P(\text{rain on at least one day}) = 1 - P(\text{rain on no days}) = 1 - (0.40 \times 0.50 + 0.40 \times 0.40) = 1 - 0.20 = 0.80
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To convert this probability to a percentage, we multiply by 100:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 0.80 \times 100 = 80\%
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the probability that it rains over the weekend, given that it rains with a \(40\%\) probability on Saturday and a \(50\%\) probability on Sunday, is \(\boxed{80}\).<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 70\%
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m [Is Boxed Matched]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m True
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m [Extracted Answer]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 80
