[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the eighth term of the geometric sequence \(3, \dfrac{9}{2}, \dfrac{27}{4}, \dfrac{81}{8}, \ldots\), we start by identifying the first term and the common ratio of the sequence.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The first term \(a\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ a = 3 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The common ratio \(r\) is the ratio of the second term to the first term:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ r = \dfrac{\frac{9}{2}}{3} = \dfrac{9}{2} \times \dfrac{1}{3} = \dfrac{3}{2} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The \(n\)-th term of a geometric sequence is given by the formula:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ a_n = a \times r^{n-1} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We want to find the eighth term (\(a_8\)):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ a_8 = 3 \times \left(\dfrac{3}{2}\right)^{8-1} = 3 \times \left(\dfrac{3}{2}\right)^7 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we calculate \(\left(\dfrac{3}{2}\right)^7\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \left(\dfrac{3}{2}\right)^7 = \dfrac{3^7}{2^7} = \dfrac{2187}{128} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, we multiply this by 3:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ a_8 = 3 \times \dfrac{2187}{128} = \dfrac{6561}{128} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the eighth term of the sequence is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \boxed{\dfrac{6561}{128}} \]<|endoftext|>
