[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the units digit of \(2^{2010}\), we can analyze the pattern in the units digits of the powers of 2. Let's start by examining the first few powers of 2:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2^1 = 2 \quad \text{(units digit is 2)}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2^2 = 4 \quad \text{(units digit is 4)}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2^3 = 8 \quad \text{(units digit is 8)}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2^4 = 16 \quad \text{(units digit is 6)}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2^5 = 32 \quad \text{(units digit is 2)}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We can see that the units digits repeat every 4 numbers: 2, 4, 8, 6. This means the units digit of \(2^n\) depends on the remainder when \(n\) is divided by 4. In other words, the units digit of \(2^{2010}\) will depend on whether \(2010 \mod 4 = 0, 1, 2, 3\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since \(2010 \mod 4 = 2\), we have \(2010 = 4 \cdot 502 + 2\). This means that \(2^{2010} = 2^2 = 4\). The units digit of 4 is 4.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the units digit of \(2^{2010}\) is \(\boxed{4}\).<|endoftext|>
