[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the area of \(\triangle DBC\), we start by noting the coordinates of the points given in the diagram. We can place point \(A\) at \((0, 6)\), point \(B\) at \((0, 0)\), point \(C\) at \((8, 0)\), point \(D\) at \((0, 3)\), and point \(E\) at \((4, 0)\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, we calculate the base and height of \(\triangle DBC\). The base of \(\triangle DBC\) is the segment \(BD\), which is the distance from \(D\) to \(B\). Since \(D\) has coordinates \((0, 3)\) and \(B\) has coordinates \((0, 0)\), the base \(BD\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m BD = 8 - 0 = 8
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The height of \(\triangle DBC\) is the vertical distance from \(D\) to \(BC\), which is the distance from \(D\) to the line \(BC\). Since \(BC\) is the line through points \(C\) and \(B\), the line \(BC\) has the equation \(y = 0\). Therefore, the height of \(\triangle DBC\) is the \(y\)-coordinate of \(D\), which is 3.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The area \(A\) of a triangle is given by the formula:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m A = \frac{1}{2} \times \text{base} \times \text{height}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Substituting the base and height of \(\triangle DBC\), we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m A = \frac{1}{2} \times 8 \times 3 = \frac{1}{2} \times 24 = 12
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the area of \(\triangle DBC\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{12}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
