[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the probability that George flips exactly 2 tails in 7 flips of an unfair coin, we can use the binomial probability formula. The binomial probability formula is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m P(X = k) = \binom{n}{k} p^k (1-p)^{n-k}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m where:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \( n \) is the number of trials (flips),
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \( k \) is the number of successful trials (tails),
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \( p \) is the probability of success on an individual trial,
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \( \binom{n}{k} \) is the binomial coefficient, which represents the number of ways to choose \( k \) successes out of \( n \) trials.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m In this problem:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \( n = 7 \) (since George flips the coin 7 times),
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \( k = 2 \) (since we want exactly 2 tails),
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \( p = \frac{3}{4} \) (since the coin has a \(\frac{3}{4}\) probability of coming up tails),
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \( 1-p = \frac{1}{4} \) (since the probability of getting heads is \(\frac{1}{4}\)).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, we calculate the binomial coefficient \(\binom{7}{2}\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \binom{7}{2} = \frac{7!}{2!(7-2)!} = \frac{7!}{2! \cdot 5!} = \frac{7 \times 6 \times 5!}{2 \times 1 \times 5!} = \frac{7 \times 6}{2 \times 1} = 21
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we calculate \( p^k \) and \( (1-p)^{n-k} \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m p^2 = \left(\frac{3}{4}\right)^2 = \frac{9}{16}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m (1-p)^{7-2} = \left(\frac{1}{4}\right)^5 = \frac{1}{1024}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, we combine these values into the binomial probability formula:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m P(X = 2) = \binom{7}{2} \left(\frac{3}{4}\right)^2 \left(\frac{1}{4}\right)^5 = 21 \times \frac{9}{16} \times \frac{1}{1024} = 21 \times \frac{9}{16384} = \frac{189}{16384}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the probability that George flips exactly 2 tails in 7 flips of the coin is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{\frac{189}{16384}}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \frac{189}{16384}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
