[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the area of the mirror, we need to find the dimensions of the mirror itself. The mirror fits exactly within the frame, and the outer perimeter of the frame measures 60 cm by 80 cm, and the width of each side of the frame is 10 cm. This creates a right-angled triangle within the frame with one leg being the width of the frame (10 cm) and the other leg being the height of the mirror.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, we calculate the area of the inner rectangle (which is the bottom portion of the mirror):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Area of the inner rectangle} = \text{outer width} \times \text{outer length} = 60 \, \text{cm} \times 80 \, \text{cm} = 4800 \, \text{cm}^2
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we calculate the area of the outer rectangle (which is the total area of the mirror including the frame):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Area of the outer rectangle} = 60 \, \text{cm} \times 80 \, \text{cm} + 2 \times \text{height of the mirror} \times \text{width of the frame}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This simplifies to:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Area of the outer rectangle} = 4800 \, \text{cm}^2 + 2 \times \text{height of the mirror} \times 10 \, \text{cm}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since the mirror fits exactly within the frame, the three sides of the inner rectangle must be equal in length. Let the height of the mirror be \( h \) cm. Then we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 60 \, \text{cm} \times 80 \, \text{cm} = 2 \times 10 \, \text{cm} \times h
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Solving for \( h \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 4800 \, \text{cm}^2 = 20 \, \text{cm} \times h
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m h = \frac{4800 \, \text{cm}^2}{20 \, \text{cm}} = 240 \, \text{cm}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, we can find the area of the mirror by adding the area of the inner rectangle and the height of the mirror:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Area of the mirror} = 4800 \, \text{cm}^2 + 240 \, \text{cm} = 4040 \, \text{cm}^2
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the area of the mirror is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{4040}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
