[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the cube root of \(2^6 \cdot 3^3 \cdot 11^3\), we start by expressing the given expression in a more convenient form. Notice that \(11^3 = (11)^3 = 1331\). Therefore, the expression can be written as:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[2^6 \cdot 3^3 \cdot 11^3 = 2^6 \cdot 3^3 \cdot 1331\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, we need to find the cube root of this expression. Using the property of exponents that the cube root of a product is the product of the cube roots, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[\sqrt[3]{2^6 \cdot 3^3 \cdot 1331} = \sqrt[3]{2^6} \cdot \sqrt[3]{3^3} \cdot \sqrt[3]{1331}\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We can simplify each cube root separately. Starting with \(\sqrt[3]{2^6}\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[\sqrt[3]{2^6} = \sqrt[3]{2^5} = \sqrt[3]{2^4 \cdot 2} = \sqrt[3]{16 \cdot 2} = \sqrt[3]{32}\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, for \(\sqrt[3]{3^3}\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[\sqrt[3]{3^3} = 3\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Finally, for \(\sqrt[3]{1331}\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[\sqrt[3]{1331} = \sqrt[3]{7^3 \cdot 11} = \sqrt[3]{7^3} \cdot \sqrt[3]{11} = 7 \sqrt[3]{11}\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Putting it all together, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[\sqrt[3]{2^6 \cdot 3^3 \cdot 1331} = \sqrt[3]{32} \cdot 3 \cdot 7 \sqrt[3]{11} = 216 \sqrt[3]{11}\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the final answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[\boxed{216 \sqrt[3]{11}}\]<|endoftext|>
