[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m If $f(x)$ is a polynomial of degree 4, and $g(x)$ is a polynomial of degree 2, then what is the degree of polynomial $f(x) - g(x)$?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the degree of the polynomial \( f(x) - g(x) \), we need to analyze the degrees of the polynomials \( f(x) \) and \( g(x) \) separately and then combine the results.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Given:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. \( f(x) \) is a polynomial of degree 4.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. \( g(x) \) is a polynomial of degree 2.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The degree of a polynomial is the highest power of the variable \( x \) that appears in the polynomial. Therefore, the degree of \( f(x) \) is 4, and the degree of \( g(x) \) is 2.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m When we subtract two polynomials, the resulting polynomial will have the same degree as the polynomial with the largest non-zero degree in the list of degrees of the polynomials being subtracted. Since the degree of \( f(x) \) is 4 and the degree of \( g(x) \) is 2, the degree of the polynomial \( f(x) - g(x) \) will be 4.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the degree of the polynomial \( f(x) - g(x) \) is \(\boxed{4}\).<|endoftext|>
