[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m In a classroom, there are 35 students and 22 bats were caught in the counting game. Each student who scored at least one home run faced最多的 questions. What is the maximum number of home runs that the board could have had? To determine the maximum number of home runs that the board could have had, we need to consider the constraints given in the problem. Specifically, for each student who scored at least one home run, there can be at most 9 questions that they answered correctly. The remaining 2 students need to have no questions answered correctly.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Let's denote the number of students who scored at least one home run by \( S \). The total number of questions that can be considered as correct for these students is \( S \). Therefore, the maximum number of home runs the board can have is \( 2 \times 9 = 18 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since there are 35 students in total and 22 bats were caught, the \( \frac{22}{3} = 7 \)th number guesses are incorrect, so 9 more bats were not caught, meaning only 13 bats were caught. Each of these 9 bats will have 0 correct answers. Therefore, each of the 9 bats must have at least one correct answer, but it is impossible to have more than one correct answer since each student who scored at least one home run must have at least one correct answer.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the maximum number of home runs the board could have had is \( 18 \).
