[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To compute \(\tan 135^\circ\), we can use the fact that the tangent function is periodic and has a known value at odd multiples of \(0^\circ, 30^\circ, 60^\circ, 90^\circ, 120^\circ,\) and \(150^\circ\). In particular, we know that \(\tan 150^\circ = -\frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since \(135^\circ\) is an odd multiple of \(0^\circ\), we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \tan 135^\circ = \tan (3 \cdot 45^\circ)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Using the known value of \(\tan 150^\circ = -\frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}\), we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \tan 135^\circ = \tan 3 \cdot 45^\circ = 3 \cdot \tan 45^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since \(\tan 45^\circ = 1\), we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \tan 135^\circ = 3 \cdot 1 = 3
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the value of \(\tan 135^\circ\) is \(\boxed{3}\).<|endoftext|>
