[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the total surface area of a hemisphere with radius 6 cm, we need to calculate the surface area of the hemisphere and add the area of its circular base. The formula for the surface area of a hemisphere is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \text{Surface Area} = 3\pi r^2 + 2\pi r^2 = 5\pi r^2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We also need to add the area of the circular base of the hemisphere. The area of a circle is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \text{Area of the circle} = \pi r^2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Given that the radius \( r \) is 6 cm, the area of the circular base is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \text{Area of the circular base} = \pi \times 6^2 = 36\pi \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, we can substitute the area of the circular base into the total surface area formula:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \text{Total Surface Area} = 5\pi r^2 + 36\pi = 5\pi (6^2) + 36\pi = 5\pi \times 36 + 36\pi = 180\pi + 36\pi = 216\pi \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the total surface area of the hemisphere is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \boxed{216\pi} \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
