[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To express $F(t)$ in terms of $\log e$ and $x$, we start by defining a new function for $F(t)$:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ q(t) = F(t) = \frac{\partial^2 f(x)}{\partial t^2} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ q(t) = \frac{\partial t f(x)}{\partial t} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We are given:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ q(t) = \frac{\partial}{\partial t} \left( \frac{\partial f}{\partial t} \right) \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 1 = \frac{\partial q}{\partial t} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Given the hard-to-solve Cauchy-Euler equation:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial t^2} + \frac{\partial f}{\partial t} = 0 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m with the initial conditions:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ u(t) = \frac{\partial f}{\partial t} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ q(t) = \frac{\partial u}{\partial t} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ u(0) = \cos(\pi x) \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ u(1) = 0 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We can use these initial and boundary conditions to eliminate the unknown function $f(x)$. First, solve for $u(x)$:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ u(x) = \int_0^x \cos(\pi t) dt = \frac{1}{2\pi} \sin(\pi x) \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Now, substitute $u(x)$ and $q(t)$ into the Cauchy-Euler equation:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \frac{\partial^2}{\partial t^2} \left( \frac{1}{2\pi} \sin(\pi x) \right) + \frac{\partial}{\partial t} \left( \frac{1}{2\pi} \sin(\pi x) \right) = 0 \]
