[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Compute $\dbinom{1293}{1}$.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To compute \(\binom{1293}{1}\), we start by recalling the definition of the binomial coefficient:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \binom{n}{k} = \frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m In this case, \(n = 1293\) and \(k = 1\). Plugging these values into the formula, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \binom{1293}{1} = \frac{1293!}{1!(1293-1)!} = \frac{1293!}{1! \cdot 1292!}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we notice that \(1293!\) (which is \(1293 \times 1292!\)) appears in the numerator and the denominator, so we can simplify the expression by canceling it out:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \binom{1293}{1} = \frac{1293 \times 1292!}{1 \times 1292!} = \frac{1293 \times 1292!}{1292!} = 1293
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the value of \(\binom{1293}{1}\) is \(\boxed{1293}\).<|endoftext|>
