[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m If a recipe calls for a ratio of 3 tablespoons of flour for every 2 eggs, how many tablespoons of flour are needed to make 10 eggs?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the amount of flour needed to make 10 eggs, given the ratio of 3 tablespoons of flour to 2 eggs, we can set up a proportion based on the given ratio.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The ratio given is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \frac{3 \text{ tablespoons}}{2 \text{ eggs}} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We need to find the amount of flour (\( x \)) required for 10 eggs. We can set up the proportion as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \frac{3 \text{ tablespoons}}{2 \text{ eggs}} = \frac{x \text{ tablespoons}}{10 \text{ eggs}} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To solve for \( x \), we cross-multiply:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 3 \text{ tablespoons} \times 10 \text{ eggs} = 2 \text{ eggs} \times x \text{ tablespoons} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This simplifies to:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 30 \text{ tablespoons} = 2x \text{ tablespoons} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we solve for \( x \) by dividing both sides of the equation by 2:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ x = \frac{30 \text{ tablespoons}}{2} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ x = 15 \text{ tablespoons} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the amount of flour needed to make 10 eggs is \(\boxed{15}\) tablespoons.
