[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Ioana has three ropes whose lengths are 39 inches, 52 inches and 65 inches. She wants to cut the ropes into equal length pieces for magic tricks. No rope is to be wasted. What is the greatest number of inches possible in the length of each piece?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the greatest number of inches possible in the length of each piece, we need to find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of the lengths of the three ropes: 39 inches, 52 inches, and 65 inches. The GCD will give us the largest number that can evenly divide these lengths.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, we find the prime factorization of each number:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - The prime factorization of 39 is \(39 = 3 \times 13\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - The prime factorization of 52 is \(52 = 2^2 \times 13\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - The prime factorization of 65 is \(65 = 5 \times 13\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The only common prime factor among 39, 52, and 65 is 13. Therefore, the greatest common divisor of 39, 52, and 65 is 13.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we divide each of these lengths by the GCD (13) to find out how many inches each piece will be:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - Each piece of rope will be \( \frac{39}{13} = 3 \) inches.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - Each piece of rope will be \( \frac{52}{13} = 4 \) inches.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - Each piece of rope will be \( \frac{65}{13} = 5 \) inches.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the greatest number of inches possible in the length of each piece is \(\boxed{13}\).<|endoftext|>
