[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To evaluate \(\lfloor 3.2 \rfloor\), we need to understand what the floor function \(\lfloor x \rfloor\) represents. The floor function, denoted by \(\lfloor x \rfloor\), gives the greatest integer less than or equal to \(x\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Given the input \(3.2\), we need to find the greatest integer that is less than or equal to \(3.2\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. Identify the integer part of \(3.2\). The integer part of \(3.2\) is the greatest integer that is less than or equal to \(3.2\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. Since \(3.2\) is between \(3\) and \(4\), the greatest integer less than or equal to \(3.2\) is \(3\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, \(\lfloor 3.2 \rfloor = 3\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
