[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the remainder when \(1999^{2000}\) is divided by 5, we can use modular arithmetic. Specifically, we need to find \(1999 \mod 5\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, let's calculate \(1999 \mod 5\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 1999 \div 5 = 399 \text{ remainder } 4 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 1999 \equiv 4 \pmod{5} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, \(1999 \equiv 4 \pmod{5}\), which means \(1999 \) leaves a remainder of 4 when divided by 5.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we need to find \(4^{2000} \mod 5\). We can use properties of exponents to simplify this. First, observe that \(4 \equiv -1 \pmod{5}\), so we can rewrite \(4^{2000}\) as:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 4^{2000} = (-1)^{2000} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since any odd power of \(-1\) is \(-1\), we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ (-1)^{2000} = 1 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore,
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 4^{2000} \equiv 1 \pmod{5} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, the remainder when \(1999^{2000}\) is divided by 5 is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \boxed{1} \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
