[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine how many non-zero digits are to the right of the decimal point in \(\frac{60}{2^3 \cdot 5^8}\), we start by simplifying the expression.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, we calculate the denominator:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[2^3 \cdot 5^8 = 8 \cdot 390625 = 3125000.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So the expression becomes:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[\frac{60}{3125000}.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we convert \(\frac{60}{3125000}\) to a decimal. We can do this by dividing 60 by 3125000:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[60 \div 3125000 \approx 2.057859 \times 10^{-5}.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This means that \(\frac{60}{3125000}\) is approximately \(2.057859 \times 10^{-5}\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The repeating decimal \(2.057859 \times 10^{-5}\) has a total of 8 non-zero digits after the decimal point. Therefore, the number of non-zero digits to the right of the decimal point is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[8.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, the final answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[\boxed{8}.\]<|endoftext|>
