[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To rationalize the denominator of $\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}-1}$, we need to eliminate the square root from the denominator. We do this by multiplying both the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. The conjugate of $\sqrt{2}-1$ is $\sqrt{2}+1$. So, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}-1} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{2}+1}{\sqrt{2}+1} = \frac{\sqrt{2}+1}{(\sqrt{2})^2 - 1^2} = \frac{\sqrt{2}+1}{2-1} = \frac{\sqrt{2}+1}{1} = \sqrt{2} + 1
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the simplified form of the expression is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{\sqrt{2}+1}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \sqrt{2}+1
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
