[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To compute \(\cos 1050^\circ\), we can use the periodicity property of the cosine function, which states that \(\cos(\theta) = \cos(\theta + 360^\circ \cdot k)\) for any integer \(k\). This means we can reduce the angle \(1050^\circ\) to an equivalent angle within the range \([0^\circ, 360^\circ)\) by subtracting multiples of \(360^\circ\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, we find how many full rotations of \(360^\circ\) fit into \(1050^\circ\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1050^\circ \div 360^\circ = 2.9167
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This means that \(1050^\circ\) is equivalent to \(2\) full rotations plus an additional \(0.9167\) of a rotation. Since \(0.9167\) is slightly more than \(360^\circ \cdot 0.003368\), we can reduce \(1050^\circ\) by subtracting \(360^\circ \cdot 0.003368\) two more times:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1050^\circ - 360^\circ \cdot 0.003368 = 1050^\circ - 10.9847^\circ = 1039.0153^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \cos 1050^\circ = \cos 1039.0153^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we need to determine the cosine of \(1039.0153^\circ\). We can use a calculator to find the value of \(\cos 1039.0153^\circ\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \cos 1039.0153^\circ \approx -0.4382
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the value of \(\cos 1050^\circ\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{-0.4382}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
