[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To solve the problem, we need to determine the remainder when the sum of three positive integers, which are divided by 47, leaves a remainder of 25 when divided by 47. This is equivalent to finding \( x \mod 47 \) where \( x = 25k + 20m + 3 \) for some integers \( k \) and \( m \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We start by considering the given information:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 25k + 20m + 3 \equiv 25 \pmod{47} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This simplifies to:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 25k + 20m \equiv 22 \pmod{47} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we need to find the congruence mod 47 for \( 25k \) and \( 20m \) separately. We do this by dividing the whole expression by 47:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 25k + 20m \equiv 22 \pmod{47} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Simplifying the coefficients modulo 47:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 25 \equiv -12 \pmod{47} \quad \text{(since \( 25 \equiv 25 - 1 \cdot 47 \pmod{47} \))} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 20 \equiv -10 \pmod{47} \quad \text{(since \( 20 \equiv 20 - 3 \cdot 47 \pmod{47} \))} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So the congruence becomes:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ -12k - 10m \equiv 22 \pmod{47} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Multiplying both sides by -1 to get a positive equivalent remainder:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 12k + 10m \equiv -22 \pmod{47} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since \(-22 \equiv 25 \pmod{47}\) (because \(-22 + 47 = 25\)), the equation becomes:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 12k + 10m \equiv 25 \pmod{47} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We now have two equations:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. \( 12k + 10m \equiv 25 \pmod{47} \)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. \( 12k + 10m \equiv -22 \pmod{47} \)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m These equations are dependent. We can use the second equation to express \( 12k + 10m \) in terms of \( 22 \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 12k + 10m = 22 + 2.5 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since \( 12k + 10m \) must be an integer, \( 2.5 \) must be an integer, which it is. Therefore:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 12k + 10m = 23.5 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This implies:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ k + m = 2 \text{ (since the integer part must be a multiple of 2)} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We can now solve the system of congruences. From the second equation, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ m = 22 + 2.5k \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This must be a non-negative integer for all integers \( k \). We substitute \( m = 22 + 2.5k \) back into the first equation:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 12k + 10(22 + 2.5k) = 25 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 12k + 220 + 25k = 25 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 37k + 220 = 25 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 37k = -195 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This is not possible since \( k \) is a positive integer. Therefore, we need to re-evaluate our approach. Instead, we solve the system directly by checking the remainders:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 12k + 10m \equiv 25 \pmod{47} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m From the second equation, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 12k + 10m = 22 + 2.5(22 + 2.5k) = 22 + 55 + 5.625k = 77 + 5.625k \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This gives us:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 12k + 10m = 77 + 5.625k + 15k = 77 + 25.625k \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Integrating, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ 12k + 10m = (15 - 0.625)k + 77 = 70k + 77 \equiv 77 \equiv 0 \pmod{47} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So the remainder is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \boxed{0} \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
