[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To convert the point \((2\sqrt{3}, \frac{2\pi}{3})\) from polar coordinates to rectangular coordinates, we use the formulas:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ x = r \cos \theta \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ y = r \sin \theta \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m where \( (r, \theta) \) are the polar coordinates and \((x, y)\) are the rectangular coordinates.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Given:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ r = 2\sqrt{3} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \theta = \frac{2\pi}{3} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, we calculate \( x \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ x = 2\sqrt{3} \cos \left( \frac{2\pi}{3} \right) \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We know that \(\cos \left( \frac{2\pi}{3} \right) = -\frac{1}{2}\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ x = 2\sqrt{3} \left( -\frac{1}{2} \right) = 2\sqrt{3} \cdot -\frac{1}{2} = -\sqrt{3} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we calculate \( y \):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ y = 2\sqrt{3} \sin \left( \frac{2\pi}{3} \right) \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We know that \(\sin \left( \frac{2\pi}{3} \right) = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ y = 2\sqrt{3} \left( \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \right) = 2\sqrt{3} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} = 2 \cdot \frac{3}{2} = 3 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the rectangular coordinates are:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \boxed{(-\sqrt{3}, 3)} \]<|endoftext|>
