[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The expected value of the box is zero when all the players have the same opinion, since each player will give the same amount of coins when they die, and all the coins paid will be received from these players. Therefore, the expected value of the game is simply the initial cash amount, which we will call \( E \).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since the players' opinions are arranged in an inverse-decreasing order, let's consider the player who died last, who has the lowest order. Let's say they have the opinion \( x_{\text{opinion1}} \). The player who died last is the player in the position that was the first to die (i.e., the player with the smallest order from the beginning), which is \( x_{\text{opinion1}} \). The player who did not die can afford to have an opinion of at most \( x_{\text{opinion1}} \) to ensure that the expected value of the game is zero.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To ensure that the expected value is close to zero, since all players have the same opinion initially, the player who died last, \( x_{\text{opinion1}} \), can afford to have an opinion such that the expected value of the game is very close to zero. Specifically, the player who died last can afford to have the opinion \( x_{\text{opinion1}} \), and the other players will have viewpoints that are too small to mitigate the expected value of the game to zero beyond the median opinion.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the number of coins that must be paid at the beginning of the game, we need to consider the players' opinions in the final arrangement. Since the problem does not specify the exact arrangement of the players, we can simplify by considering the worst-case scenario where all players have the same opinion. In this case, the worst player who died last will have a large, but finite, opinion value of zero coins paid to ensure the expected value of the game is zero.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m However, to generalize, we need to ensure that the expected value is close to zero. Since the problem is symmetric and all players have the same initial order, the player who died last will have an opinion sufficiently large to ensure that the expected value is close to zero. The number of coins the player who died last will have, but could be further determined by the exact dividing, will be enough to achieve an expected value of zero.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the number of coins that must be paid at the beginning of the game so as to make the expected value of the game zero is \( n \). The answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{n}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
