[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Jenna is at a fair with four friends. They all want to ride the roller coaster, but only three people can fit in a car. How many different groups of three can the five of them make?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine how many different groups of three can be made from five people, we need to calculate the number of combinations of 5 people taken 3 at a time. The formula for combinations is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \binom{n}{r} = \frac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Here, \( n = 5 \) and \( r = 3 \). Plugging these values into the formula, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \binom{5}{3} = \frac{5!}{3!(5-3)!} = \frac{5!}{3!2!}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we need to calculate the factorials. Recall that \( n! \) (n factorial) is the product of all positive integers up to \( n \). So,
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 5! = 5 \times 4 \times 3 \times 2 \times 1 = 120
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3! = 3 \times 2 \times 1 = 6
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2! = 2 \times 1 = 2
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Substituting these values back into the combination formula, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \binom{5}{3} = \frac{120}{6 \times 2} = \frac{120}{12} = 10
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the number of different groups of three that can be made from five people is \(\boxed{10}\).<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 10
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m [Is Boxed Matched]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m True
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
