[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the matrix that corresponds to rotating about the origin by an angle of \(45^\circ\) clockwise, we need to understand the rotation matrix and how it transforms vectors.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The general form of a 3x3 rotation matrix \(R\) that rotates around the origin by an angle \(\theta\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ R = \begin{pmatrix} \cos \theta & -\sin \theta & 0 \\ \sin \theta & \cos \theta & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m For a \(45^\circ\) clockwise rotation, \(\theta = 45^\circ\). The cosine and sine of 45 degrees are:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \cos 45^\circ = \sin 45^\circ = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the rotation matrix \(R\) for a \(45^\circ\) clockwise rotation is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ R = \begin{pmatrix} \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 \\ \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} & \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Putting this matrix in the box, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \boxed{\begin{pmatrix} \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 \\ \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} & \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}} \]<|endoftext|>
