[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To factor the expression \(45x + 30\), we need to find the greatest common factor (GCF) of the terms. In this case, both terms are multiples of 30.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The GCF of 45 and 30 is 15. Therefore, we can factor out 15 from the expression.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m So, we can write:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[45x + 30 = 15(3x) + 15(2)\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m This simplifies to:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[45x + 30 = 15(3x + 2)\]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the factorized form of the expression is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[\boxed{15(3x + 2)}\]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m [Ground Truth]
