[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the number of ways to arrange the letters of the word "PROOF," we need to calculate the number of permutations of the multiset {P, R, O, F}. The word "PROOF" consists of 4 letters where P appears 1 time, R appears 1 time, O appears 1 time, and F appears 1 time.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The formula for the number of permutations of a multiset is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \frac{n!}{n_1! \times n_2! \times \ldots \times n_k!}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m where \(n\) is the total number of items to arrange, and \(n_1, n_2, \ldots, n_k\) are the frequencies of the distinct items.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m For the word "PROOF":
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \(n = 4\) (total letters)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \(n_P = 1\) (P appears 1 time)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \(n_R = 1\) (R appears 1 time)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \(n_O = 1\) (O appears 1 time)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \(n_F = 1\) (F appears 1 time)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Plugging these values into the formula, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \frac{4!}{1! \times 1! \times 1! \times 1!} = \frac{4!}{1!} = \frac{24}{1} = 24
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the number of ways to arrange the letters of the word "PROOF" is \(\boxed{24}\).<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 60
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
