[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Great! Let's solve this problem step by step using geometric properties and vector geometry.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Given:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - Triangle \(ABC\) is inscribed in an equilateral triangle \(PQR\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - \(PC = 3\) and \(BP = CQ = 2\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m - We need to find \(AQ\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ### Step 1: Understand the Geometry
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since \(PC = 3\) and \(BP = 2\), we can use some properties of equilateral triangles and their supplements. Note that \(P\) and \(Q\) are points on \(PQ\) and \(PR\) respectively, and since \(ABC\) is equilateral, \(BC = CA = AB\). Also, \(PQ\) and \(PR\) are segments connecting the points to \(P\) and \(Q\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ### Step 2: Use Symmetry and Equal Segments
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Because \(PQR\) is an equilateral triangle, the segment \(AB\) is split into three equal parts due to the symmetry of the equilateral triangle. This means \(PQ = PR = QB = QP = RC = QR = RC = QB = QP = PR = x\). Therefore, each part of \(AB\) is \(x\), and the total length of \(AB\) is \(3x\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ### Step 3: Use Vector Geometry
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find \(AQ\), we can use the fact that \(ABC\) is equilateral and then find the position of \(A\) relative to \(P\) and \(Q\). Since \(P\) and \(Q\) are on \(PQ\) and \(PR\) respectively, and the equilateral nature of the triangle, we can use vector geometry to determine the coordinates of \(A\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since \(PQ\) is parallel to \(BC\) and \(PQ\) is split into three equal segments by \(CQ\), we can determine the direction vector vector \(\overrightarrow{PC} = 3\) and \(\overrightarrow{BC} = \resize{Vareq} 3\hat{i}\), where \(\hat{i}\) is a unit vector along \(BC\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since \(P\) is \(2\) units from \(Q\), the vector \(\overrightarrow{PQ}\) can be determined as \([-2, 3]\) because \(P\) is 2 units from \(Q\) and \(Q\) is on \(PR\). Since \(P\) and \(Q\) are on \(PQ\) and \(PR\) respectively, the length of \(PQ\) is the same as \(PR\), so \(PQ = x\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The vector \(\overrightarrow{PQ}\) can be expressed as \([-2, 3] = x\hat{j}\). Since \(\overrightarrow{PQ} = \overrightarrow{AC} + \overrightarrow{BQ}\), we can decompose it into components along the \(y\)-axis and the \(x\)-axis.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Vectors along the \(x\)-axis and \(y\)-axis are \([-2, 0]\) and \([0, 3]\) respectively. Since \(PQ\) is split into three equal parts, we can infer that \(PQ\) lies along the line connecting \(P\) and \(Q\), and thus \(\overrightarrow{PQ} = -2\hat{i} + 3\hat{j}\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since \(P\) is 2 units from \(Q\), and \(PQ\) is split into three equal segments, \(\overrightarrow{PQ}\) is along the \(x\)-axis and \(\overrightarrow{PQ} = -2\hat{i} + 3\hat{j}\). The vector \(\overrightarrow{PQ}\) can be written as \(2\hat{i}\), so \(-2\hat{i} + 3\hat{j} = 2\hat{i}\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, \(\hat{i} = 1\) and \(\hat{j} = 3\), or \(x = 3\). Since \(PC = 3\hat{i}\), the projection of \(P\) onto \(BC\) is along the \(x\)-axis, so \(AQ = 3\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m ### Final Answer
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \boxed{3}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
