[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To determine the number of paths from \(A\) to \(B\) using only moves that are either to the right (R) or up (U), we need to consider the following:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. From \(A\) to \(B\), we need to make a total of 6 moves (4 R and 2 U). This is because \(B\) is at row 5 (since \(w = 6\)) and we need 2 more moves to get to the end (right and up).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. We can think of this as choosing 2 positions out of the 6 for the R moves (or equivalently, for the U moves). The number of ways to choose 2 positions out of 6 is given by the combination formula \(\binom{n}{k}\), which represents the number of ways to choose \(k\) elements from a set of \(n\) elements without regard to the order of selection.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The combination formula is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \binom{n}{k} = \frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m In this case, \(n = 6\) and \(k = 2\), so we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \binom{6}{2} = \frac{6!}{2!(6-2)!} = \frac{6!}{2!4!} = \frac{6 \times 5 \times 4!}{2 \times 1 \times 4!} = \frac{6 \times 5}{2 \times 1} = 15
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the number of paths from \(A\) to \(B\) is \(\boxed{15}\).<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
