[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \sin x = \sin 70^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    The sine function is symmetric about \( x = 90^\circ \), and \(\sin 70^\circ\) represents the y-coordinate of the point on the sine curve corresponding to \( x = 70^\circ \). Therefore, the curve \( y = \sin x \) intersects the horizontal line \( y = \sin 70^\circ \) at:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    x = 70^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. **Determine the lengths of the segments cut by the horizontal line:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    The horizontal line \( y = \sin 70^\circ \) cuts the curve into two segments of equal length. Therefore, the lengths of these segments are both:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \sin 70^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    which can be calculated as:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \sin 70^\circ = 0.940 \quad \text{and} \quad \sin 40^\circ = 0.6428
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 3. **Form the successive ratios:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    The successive ratios given are:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    p : q : p : q : \cdots
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    This means the segments form a sequence where the length of the segment \( p \) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    p = \sin 70^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    and the length of the segment \( q \) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    q = \sin 40^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    We need to find the ordered pair \((p, q)\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 4. **Calculate the products \( p \) and \( q \):**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    p = \sin 70^\circ \approx 0.940
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    q = \sin 40^\circ \approx 0.6428
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 5. **Compute the ordered pair \((p, q)\):**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    Since \(\sin 70^\circ\) and \(\sin 40^\circ\) are very close to \(\sin 45^\circ\), and given their trigonometric values, we can approximate:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    (p, q) \approx (0.940, 0.6428)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 6. **Express the order as a fraction:**
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    The ordered pair \((p, q)\) can be written as:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    (p, q) = \left( 0.940, 0.6428 \right)
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    To check for relative prime numbers, we need to see if \(\gcd(p, q) = 1\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \gcd(0.940, 0.6428) = 1
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    Therefore, the order \((p, q)\) is already in its simplest form.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Thus, the ordered pair of relatively prime positive integers \((p, q)\) is:
