[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the number of real roots of the equation \(\begin{vmatrix} x & c & -b \\ -c & x & a \\ b & -a & x \end{vmatrix} = 0\), we start by calculating the determinant of the given matrix. The determinant of a \(3 \times 3\) matrix \(\begin{vmatrix} a & b & c \\ d & e & f \\ g & h & i \end{vmatrix}\) is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m a(ei - fh) - b(di - fg) + c(dh - eg).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m For our matrix, this becomes:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \begin{vmatrix} x & c & -b \\ -c & x & a \\ b & -a & x \end{vmatrix} = x(xx - a(-a)) - c(-xc - ba) + (-b)(-cx + cab).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Simplifying the expression inside the determinant, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m x(x^2 + a^2) + c(xa - a^2) + b(cx - cab) = x^3 + a^2x + cxa - ac^2 + bch - cabx = x^3 + a^2x + cxa - ac^2 + bch - cabx.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Combining like terms, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m x^3 + a^2x + cxa - ac^2 + bch - cabx.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Factor out \(x\) and \(a\) from the terms involving \(x\):
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m x(x^2 + a^2) + ac(a - b)x - ac^2 + bch = x(x^2 + a^2) + ac(a - b)(x - c) - ac^2 + bch.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Notice that \(a^2 = a\) and \((a - b)(x - c) = ax - ac - bx + bc = (a - c)x - ac = (a - c)(x - c) - ac = (a - c)x - ac - bx + bc = (a - c)(x - c) - ac = (a - c)(x - c) + bc = (a - c)(x - c) - bc = (a - c)(x - c) - bc\), but we can simplify further by recognizing that the terms involving \(x\) and \(a\) cancel out, leaving us with:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m x^3 + a^2x + bch = x^3 + a^2x + bch.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Since \(b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i\) are nonzero real numbers, the expression simplifies to:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m x^3 + a^2x + bch.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m For the determinant to be zero, the expression must not have any real roots. This means that the polynomial \(x^3 + a^2x + bch\) must not have any real roots. Since \(a^2 = a\), the expression becomes:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m x^3 + a^2x + bch = x^3 + ax + bch.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The cubic polynomial \(x^3 + ax + bch\) can have at most three real roots. However, we need to check if it can have exactly three real roots. Since \(a^2 = a\) and the polynomial is of degree 3, it can have up to three real roots by the symmetric polynomial theorem, but we need to ensure that it has exactly three real roots.
