[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m A cube of cheese
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[C = \{(x,y,z) : 0 \le x, y, z \le 1\}\]is cut along the planes $x = y,$ $y = z,$ and $z = x.$  How many pieces are there?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To solve the problem of cutting a cube of cheese along the planes \(x = y\), \(y = z\), and \(z = x\) and finding the number of pieces, we need to consider the different combinations of these cuts.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 1. **Cutting along the planes \(x = y\) and \(y = z\)**:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - This cuts the cube along a diagonal, which will not result in any new pieces because the slices are not flat. So, there are no new cubes.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 2. **Cutting along the plane \(y = z\) and \(z = x\)**:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - This cuts the cube into two parts.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The first part is along the plane \(x = y\), which is the same as the first plane \(x = y\) but repeated along the other axes.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - The second part is along the plane \(z = x\), which is the same as the second plane \(z = x\) but repeated along the other axes.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m    - These cuts will also result in two new pieces.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      The piece from the original cube on the \(x\)-\(y\) plane will be split into two new pieces along the \(y\)-\(z\) plane. Similarly, the piece from the \(x\)-\(z\) plane on the \(y\)-\(z\) plane will be split into two new pieces along the \(z\)-\(x\) plane.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m      Therefore, the total number of pieces is \(2 + 2 = 4\).
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The final answer is \(\boxed{4}\).<|endoftext|>
