[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find \(\sin{X}\) in the right triangle \(XYZ\), we need to use the definition of sine in a right triangle. In a right triangle, the sine of an angle is the ratio of the length of the opposite side to the length of the hypotenuse. However, we can also express \(\sin{X}\) using the sine of the angle opposite to \(X\), which is the angle at the right angle.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, let's identify the sides of the triangle. We have a right triangle with a right angle at \(X\), and the side opposite to \(X\) is the leg of length 8, which is adjacent to \(X\). The side adjacent to \(X\) (along with \(X\)) is the hypotenuse of the right triangle.
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The hypotenuse \(c\) of the triangle can be found using the Pythagorean theorem:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ c = \sqrt{8^2 + 10^2} = \sqrt{64 + 100} = \sqrt{164} = 2\sqrt{41} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m The sine of angle \(X\) is the ratio of the length of the side adjacent to \(X\) to the length of the hypotenuse:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \sin{X} = \frac{\text{adjacent}}{\text{hypotenuse}} = \frac{8}{2\sqrt{41}} = \frac{4}{\sqrt{41}} = \frac{4\sqrt{41}}{41} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Therefore, the value of \(\sin{X}\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[ \boxed{\frac{4\sqrt{41}}{41}} \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
