[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m A right square pyramid's base has edges measuring 20 feet each. If the pyramid's height is 27 feet, what is its volume, in cubic feet?
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m To find the volume of a right square pyramid, we can use the formula:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Volume} = \frac{1}{3} \times \text{Base Area} \times \text{Height}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m First, we need to determine the area of the base. Since the base is a square with each side measuring 20 feet, the area of the base is:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Base Area} = 20 \times 20 = 400 \text{ square feet}
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m Next, we know the height of the pyramid is 27 feet. Now we can substitute the base area and the height into the volume formula:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Volume} = \frac{1}{3} \times 400 \times 27
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m We can simplify this calculation step by step:
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 400 \times 27 = 10800
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1419827)[0m \text{Volume} = \frac{1}{3} \times 10800 = 3600 \text{ cubic feet}
